      "generated_answer": "To find the value of the double sum $S = \\sum_{j=1}^\\infty \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j+k)^3}$, we let $n = j+k$. For a fixed $n$, the possible values for $j$ are $1, 2, \\dots, n-1$, meaning there are $n-1$ pairs of $(j, k)$ for each $n \\ge 2$.\n\nThe sum can be rewritten as:\n\\[ S = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{n-1}{n^3} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{n}{n^3} - \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right) = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3}. \\]\nUsing the definitions $p = \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^2}$ and $q = \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3}$, we observe that $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = p - 1$ and $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3} = q - 1$.\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for $S$:\n\\[ S = (p - 1) - (q - 1) = p - q. \\]\n\n\\boxed{p-q}",
