  {
    "global index": "MATH_479",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem and provide the final answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There are finitely many primes $p$ for which the congruence $$8x\\equiv 1\\pmod{{p}}$$has no solutions $x$. Determine the sum of all such $p$.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the primes $p$ for which the congruence $8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}$ has no solutions, we examine the properties of linear congruences. A linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\pmod{n}$ has a solution if and only if the greatest common divisor $\\gcd(a, n)$ divides $b$.\n\nIn this case, the congruence is $8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}$. For this to have no solution, $\\gcd(8, p)$ must not divide 1. Since the only positive divisor of 1 is 1, this means we must have $\\gcd(8, p) > 1$. Because $p$ is a prime number and the divisors of 8 are 1, 2, 4, and 8, the only prime $p$ that satisfies $\\gcd(8, p) > 1$ is $p = 2$.\n\nFor $p=2$, the congruence becomes $8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}$, which simplifies to $0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}$, a contradiction. For any other prime $p > 2$, $\\gcd(8, p) = 1$, which always divides 1, ensuring a solution exists. Thus, the only such prime is $p = 2$.\n\nThe sum of all such primes is 2.\n\n\\boxed{2}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 106,
        "output_tokens": 294,
