      "generated_answer": "To find the units digit of $4\\$$, we first calculate $4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24$.\nThe superfactorial $4\\$$ is defined as a power tower of $24$ with a height of $24$:\n$$4\\$ = \\underbrace{24^{24^{\\cdot^{\\cdot^{\\cdot^{24}}}}}}_{24}$$\nThe units digit of any power of a number ending in $4$ depends on whether the exponent is even or odd: $4^1 = 4$, $4^2 = 16$, $4^3 = 64$, $4^4 = 256$, and so on. Specifically, if the exponent is odd, the units digit is $4$; if the exponent is even, the units digit is $6$.\nIn the expression $24^k$ where $k = 24^{24^{\\cdot^{\\cdot^{\\cdot^{24}}}}}$, the exponent $k$ is clearly an even number (as it is a power of 24), so the units digit of $4\\$$ must be $6$.\n\n\\boxed{6}",
