    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we first seat the 4 people who are not Pierre, Rosa, or Thomas around the round table. Since seating arrangements that are rotations of each other are considered the same, there are $(4-1)! = 3! = 6$ ways to seat these 4 people. \n\nThese 4 people create 4 distinct gaps between them where Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas can be placed. To ensure no two of them sit next to each other, we must choose 3 of these 4 gaps and arrange the 3 people in them. The number of ways to do this is $P(4, 3) = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 = 24$.\n\nThe total number of ways is the product of these two values: $6 \\times 24 = 144$.\n\n\\boxed{144}",
