    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In a striated muscle sarcomere, the sliding filament theory dictates that the A band remains constant in length while the I band and H zone shorten. Tropomyosin is a regulatory protein that, in a relaxed state, physically blocks the myosin-binding sites on the actin (thin) filament. Therefore, muscle contraction can only occur when calcium binds to troponin, shifting tropomyosin away from these sites.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
