    "global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3285",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose thecodonsfor amino acids consisted of only two bases rather than three. Would there be a sufficient number ofcodonsfor all twenty amino acids? Show how you obtain your answer.\n\nOptions: \nA. There will be more than 20 codons for 20 amino acids\nB. 4 codons will be enough to code for all 20 amino acids, with some sharing multiple codons\nC. 16 codons will be insufficient to code for all 20 amino acids\nD. Each amino acid will have multiple codons, with a total of 40 codons available\nE. There will be exactly one codon for each amino acid, with no redundancy\nF. 25 codons will be available, providing extra combinations for some amino acids\nG. Each amino acid will have its own unique codon\nH. 16 codons will be enough to code for all 20 amino acids\nI. There will be 64 codons, providing ample combinations for all amino acids and stop signals\nJ. 20 codons will be just enough to code for each amino acid uniquely\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
