    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the electronic configurations of the ions, we look at the atomic numbers of Silicon (Si) and Oxygen (O).\n\n1.  **Silicon (Si):** The atomic number is 14, so the neutral configuration is $1s^2 2s^2 2p^6 3s^2 3p^2$. To form the $\\text{Si}^{4+}$ ion, it loses the four outermost valence electrons ($3s^2 3p^2$), leaving the configuration $1s^2 2s^2 2p^6$ (matching Neon).\n2.  **Oxygen (O):** The atomic number is 8, so the neutral configuration is $1s^2 2s^2 2p^4$. To form the $\\text{O}^{2-}$ ion, it gains two electrons to complete its valence shell, resulting in the configuration $1s^2 2s^2 2p^6$ (also matching Neon).\n\nComparing these results to the options provided, Option A matches both configurations.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
