    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_4362",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Estimate the zero-point entropy of NO.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.98 cal mol^-1 K^-1\nB. 1.10 cal mol^-1 K^-1\nC. 3.20 cal mol^-1 K^-1\nD. 2.50 cal mol^-1 K^-1\nE. 0.00 cal mol^-1 K^-1\nF. 1.75 cal mol^-1 K^-1\nG. 0.69 cal mol^-1 K^-1\nH. 2.78 cal mol^-1 K^-1\nI. 4.00 cal mol^-1 K^-1\nJ. 1.38 cal mol^-1 K^-1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To estimate the zero-point entropy (residual entropy) of a molecule, we use the Boltzmann formula:\n$S_0 = R \\ln(W)$\nwhere $R$ is the gas constant ($1.987 \\text{ cal mol}^{-1}\\text{K}^{-1}$) and $W$ is the number of possible orientations of the molecule in the crystal lattice. \n\nFor Nitric Oxide ($\\text{NO}$), which is a heteronuclear diatomic molecule with a very small dipole moment, the molecules can be oriented in two ways ($\\text{NO}$ or $\\text{ON}$) in the solid state, leading to $W = 2$.\n$S_0 = 1.987 \\times \\ln(2) \\approx 1.987 \\times 0.693 \\approx 1.376 \\text{ cal mol}^{-1}\\text{K}^{-1}$.\n\nThis value corresponds to approximately $1.38 \\text{ cal mol}^{-1}\\text{K}^{-1}$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
