    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3989",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Show that l'Hôpital's rule amounts to forming a Taylor expansion of both the numerator and the denominator. Evaluate the limit\n$$\n\\lim _{{x \\rightarrow 0}} \\frac{{\\ln (1+x)-x}}{{x^2}}\n$$\nboth ways.\n\nOptions: \nA. 3/2\nB. 0\nC. -3/2\nD. 2\nE. 1/4\nF. 1\nG. -1\nH.  -1/2\nI. 1/2\nJ. -2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the limit $\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\ln(1+x) - x}{x^2}$:\n\n1.  **Using l'Hôpital's Rule**: Since the limit is an indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$, we differentiate the numerator and denominator:\n    $$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\frac{1}{1+x} - 1}{2x} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{1 - (1+x)}{2x(1+x)} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{-x}{2x(1+x)} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{-1}{2(1+x)} = -\\frac{1}{2}$$\n2.  **Using Taylor Expansion**: The Taylor series for $\\ln(1+x)$ around $x=0$ is $x - \\frac{x^2}{2} + \\frac{x^3}{3} - \\dots$. Substituting this into the limit:\n    $$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{(x - \\frac{x^2}{2} + \\frac{x^3}{3} - \\dots) - x}{x^2} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{-\\frac{x^2}{2} + \\frac{x^3}{3} - \\dots}{x^2} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{x}{3} - \\dots\\right) = -\\frac{1}{2}$$\n\nBoth methods yield the result $-1/2$.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
