    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3785",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following operators isHermitian; d/dx,id/dx, d^2/dx^2?\n\nOptions: \nA. none are Hermitian\nB. b and c are Hermitan\nC. only a is Hermitian\nD. all are Hermitian\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine if an operator $\\hat{A}$ is Hermitian, it must satisfy the condition $\\int \\psi^* (\\hat{A}\\phi) dx = \\int (\\hat{A}\\psi)^* \\phi dx$.\n\n1.  **$d/dx$**: Integrating by parts shows that $\\int \\psi^* \\frac{d\\phi}{dx} dx = -\\int \\frac{d\\psi^*}{dx} \\phi dx$, making it anti-Hermitian.\n2.  **$i d/dx$**: The factor of $i$ flips the sign of the anti-Hermitian operator $d/dx$, making $i d/dx$ Hermitian (this is proportional to the momentum operator).\n3.  **$d^2/dx^2$**: Integrating by parts twice returns the original operator with a positive sign, making $d^2/dx^2$ Hermitian (this is proportional to the kinetic energy operator).\n\nTherefore, both $i d/dx$ and $d^2/dx^2$ are Hermitian.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
