    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_4421",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suggest a reason why liquid hydrogen fluoride is preferable to concentrated, aqueoushydrobromicacid in inducing propeneto react with benzene to produceisopropylbenzene.\n\nOptions: \nA. Hydrogen fluoride reacts more selectively with propene\nB. Hydrogen fluoride provides a better solvent environment for the reaction\nC. Hydrogen fluoride is less acidic\nD. Hydrogen fluoride has lower molecular weight, which speeds up the reaction\nE. Hydrogen fluoride will give a greater yield of product\nF. Hydrogen fluoride can be recycled more easily in the process\nG. Hydrogen fluoride is a stronger oxidizing agent\nH. Hydrogen fluoride is less reactive\nI. Hydrogen fluoride is more stable\nJ. Hydrogen fluoride has a higher boiling point, which aids in the reaction\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In the alkylation of benzene with propene (a Friedel-Crafts alkylation), liquid hydrogen fluoride (HF) acts as both a catalyst and a solvent that minimizes side reactions. Unlike aqueous hydrobromic acid, which contains water that can react with the propene to form alcohols or cause other competing reactions, HF reacts more selectively to facilitate the formation of the isopropyl cation. Therefore, the selectivity toward the desired isopropylbenzene product is the primary advantage.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
