    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3976",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: During the study of the reaction A → 2B, a chemist constructs several graphs. The graph of [A] versus time and the graph of ln [A] versus time both give a curved line; however, the graph of 1/[A] versus time and gives a straight line. This implies the rate law is\n\nOptions: \nA. Rate = k[A]^4\nB. Rate = k[A]^-1\nC. Rate = k[A]^2\nD. Rate = k[A]\nE. Rate = k[A]^-2\nF. Rate = k[A]^0\nG. Rate = k[A]^1/2\nH. Rate = k[A]^-3\nI. Rate = 2k[A]\nJ. Rate = k[A]^3\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the order of the reaction, we examine which plot of concentration versus time yields a straight line:\n1. A plot of $[A]$ vs. time is linear for **zero-order** reactions.\n2. A plot of $\\ln[A]$ vs. time is linear for **first-order** reactions.\n3. A plot of $1/[A]$ vs. time is linear for **second-order** reactions.\n\nSince the graph of $1/[A]$ versus time gives a straight line, the reaction is second-order with respect to A, meaning the rate law is $\\text{Rate} = k[A]^2$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
