  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3888",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An experiment was performed to determine the moles of carbon dioxide gas formed (collected over water) when an acid reacts with limestone. To do this, a piece of dry limestone was weighed. Then carbon dioxide was collected until the limestone disappeared. The atmospheric pressure was measured at 0.965 atm. The temperature was 295 K. The volume of CO2 was measured to the nearest mL and corrected for the vapor pressure of water. The student continuously got low results from what was expected. Can you suggest why?\n\nOptions: \nA. Perhaps there was too much acid, which interfered with the results.\nB. The measurement of atmospheric pressure was incorrect.\nC. Carbon dioxide is rather soluble in water.\nD. The temperature might have affected the reaction.\nE. Limestone is Ca(HCO3)2.\nF. The limestone did not fully disappear.\nG. Limestone is never pure CaCO3.\nH. Perhaps there was not enough acid to dissolve all the limestone.\nI. The volume of CO2 was not measured correctly.\nJ. The vapor pressure of water was not correctly accounted for.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The student is collecting carbon dioxide gas over water. Because $\\text{CO}_2$ is significantly more soluble in water than many other gases, a portion of the gas dissolves in the water instead of being collected in the measuring vessel. This leads to a measured volume lower than the theoretical yield.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
