    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_4070",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The vibrational wavenumber of $\\mathrm{{Br}}_2$ is $323.2 \\mathrm{{~cm}}^{{-1}}$. Evaluate the vibrational partition function explicitly (without approximation) and plot its value as a function of temperature. At what temperature is the value within 5 per cent of the value calculated from the approximate formula?\n\nOptions: \nA. 7000 K\nB. 2500 K\nC. 3500$\\mathrm{{~K}}$\nD. 6000 K\nE. 2000 K\nF.  4500$\\mathrm{{~K}}$\nG. 4000$\\mathrm{{~K}}$\nH. 5500 K\nI. 5000$\\mathrm{{~K}}$\nJ. 3000 K\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the temperature at which the explicit vibrational partition function $q_{vib} = \\frac{1}{1 - e^{-hc\\tilde{\\nu}/kT}}$ is within 5% of the high-temperature approximation $q_{approx} = \\frac{kT}{hc\\tilde{\\nu}}$, we set up the relative error equation:\n$$\\frac{q_{approx} - q_{vib}}{q_{vib}} = \\frac{kT}{hc\\tilde{\\nu}} (1 - e^{-hc\\tilde{\\nu}/kT}) - 1 \\approx 0.05$$\nLet $x = \\frac{hc\\tilde{\\nu}}{kT}$. The condition is $\\frac{1}{x}(1 - e^{-x}) \\approx 1 - 0.05 = 0.95$ (or similar depending on which value is the reference). For the approximation $\\frac{1}{x}$ to be within 5% of the exact value $\\frac{1}{1-e^{-x}}$, the exponent $x$ must be small. Using the Taylor expansion $e^{-x} \\approx 1 - x + \\frac{x^2}{2}$, we have $\\frac{1}{x}(1 - (1 - x + \\frac{x^2}{2})) = 1 - \\frac{x}{2}$.\nSetting $1 - \\frac{x}{2} \\approx 0.95$ gives $x \\approx 0.1$.\nCalculating $T$: $T = \\frac{hc\\tilde{\\nu}}{k(0.1)} = \\frac{(6.626 \\times 10^{-34})(3 \\times 10^{10})(323.2)}{(1.38 \\times 10^{-23})(0.1)} \\approx 4645 \\text{ K}$.\nAmong the options provided, 4500 K is the closest standard value derived from this convergence criteria.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
