    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine for which integers $n$ ($3 \\le n \\le 11$) there is only one group of order $n$ up to isomorphism, we examine each value:\n- If $n$ is prime ($3, 5, 7, 11$), there is only one group: the cyclic group $C_n$.\n- If $n = 4$, there are two groups: $C_4$ and $V_4$ (Klein four-group).\n- If $n = 6$, there are two groups: $C_6$ and $S_3$ (or $D_3$).\n- If $n = 8$, there are five groups ($C_8, C_4 \\times C_2, C_2 \\times C_2 \\times C_2, D_4, Q_8$).\n- If $n = 9$, there are two groups: $C_9$ and $C_3 \\times C_3$.\n- If $n = 10$, there are two groups: $C_{10}$ and $D_5$.\n\nThe only integers in the range where there is a unique group are the prime numbers: 3, 5, 7, and 11.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
