    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the upper critical value $z$ for a 94% confidence interval, we first determine the area in the tails. Since the confidence level is 94% (0.94), the remaining area is $1 - 0.94 = 0.06$. Because the interval is symmetric, each tail contains half of that area: $0.06 / 2 = 0.03$. \n\nWe look for the z-score that corresponds to a cumulative area of $0.94 + 0.03 = 0.97$ (or an area of 0.03 in the upper tail). Using a standard normal distribution table or calculator, $P(Z < 1.88) \\approx 0.9699$ and $P(Z < 1.881) \\approx 0.97$. Among the given options, 1.88 is the closest critical value.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
