  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7477",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For a parametric family $\\{{p_\\theta(x)\\}}$ we know that $\\lim_{{\\theta'\\to\\theta}}\\frac{{1}}{{(\\theta-\\theta')^2}}D(p_\\theta||p_{{\\theta'}}) = \\alpha J(\\theta)$, where $J(\\theta)$ is the Fisher information. Use natural logarithm for KL divergence to compute $\\alpha$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.25\nB. 4\nC. 0.5\nD. 1\nE. 2.5\nF. -1\nG. 2\nH. 1.5\nI. 3\nJ. 0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the value of $\\alpha$, we look at the Taylor expansion of the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence $D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta'})$ around $\\theta' = \\theta$.\n\nThe KL divergence is defined as $D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta'}) = \\mathbb{E}_{p_\\theta} [\\ln p_\\theta(x) - \\ln p_{\\theta'}(x)]$. The first-order derivative with respect to $\\theta'$ at $\\theta' = \\theta$ is zero, and the second-order derivative is the Fisher information $J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{p_\\theta} [(\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial \\theta} \\ln p_\\theta(x))^2]$.\n\nUsing the Taylor expansion:\n$D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta'}) \\approx D(p_\\theta || p_\\theta) + \\frac{\\partial D}{\\partial \\theta'}\\big|_{\\theta'=\\theta}(\\theta' - \\theta) + \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{\\partial^2 D}{\\partial (\\theta')^2}\\big|_{\\theta'=\\theta}(\\theta' - \\theta)^2$\n$D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta'}) \\approx 0 + 0 + \\frac{1}{2} J(\\theta) (\\theta - \\theta')^2$\n\nThus, $\\lim_{\\theta' \\to \\theta} \\frac{1}{(\\theta - \\theta')^2} D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta'}) = \\frac{1}{2} J(\\theta)$, which implies $\\alpha = 0.5$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
