    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6917",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: compute the integral \\int_{{\\Gamma}} \\frac{{x*dy-y*dx}}{{x^2+y^2}}, where $\\Gamma$ is any piecewise smooth, closed curve that encloses the origin but does not pass through it.\n\nOptions: \nA. 3.1415926\nB. 4.6692016\nC. 1.5707963\nD. 0\nE. 2.7182818\nF. 0.5772156\nG. -3.1415926\nH. 9.8696044\nI. 6.2831852\nJ. 1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the integral $\\int_{\\Gamma} \\frac{x dy - y dx}{x^2 + y^2}$, we can recognize that the integrand is the differential form of the polar angle $\\theta$. \n\nSpecifically, in polar coordinates $x = r \\cos\\theta$ and $y = r \\sin\\theta$, we have:\n$dx = \\cos\\theta dr - r \\sin\\theta d\\theta$\n$dy = \\sin\\theta dr + r \\cos\\theta d\\theta$\nSubstituting these into the expression:\n$x dy - y dx = (r \\cos\\theta)(\\sin\\theta dr + r \\cos\\theta d\\theta) - (r \\sin\\theta)(\\cos\\theta dr - r \\sin\\theta d\\theta) = r^2(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta)d\\theta = r^2 d\\theta$.\nThe denominator is $x^2 + y^2 = r^2$. Thus, the integral becomes:\n$\\int_{\\Gamma} \\frac{r^2 d\\theta}{r^2} = \\int_{\\Gamma} d\\theta$.\n\nSince $\\Gamma$ is a closed curve that encloses the origin exactly once, the total change in the angle $\\theta$ as one traverses the curve is $2\\pi$.\n$2\\pi \\approx 2 \\times 3.14159265 = 6.2831853$.\n\nComparing this to the options:\nI. 6.2831852\n\n\\boxed{I}",
