    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7357",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Compute the real integral $I=\\int_{{-\\infty}}^{{\\infty}} 1/(x^2 + 1)^2 dx$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 6.28\nB. 5.55\nC. 3.67\nD. 2.78\nE. 3.14\nF. 2.46\nG. 1.57\nH. 4.67\nI. 1.23\nJ. 0.78\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To compute the integral $I = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{(x^2+1)^2} dx$, we can use the method of residue calculus or a trigonometric substitution.\n\nUsing the substitution $x = \\tan(\\theta)$, $dx = \\sec^2(\\theta) d\\theta$, the integral becomes:\n$I = \\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sec^2(\\theta)}{(\\tan^2(\\theta)+1)^2} d\\theta = \\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sec^2(\\theta)}{\\sec^4(\\theta)} d\\theta = \\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\cos^2(\\theta) d\\theta$.\nUsing the identity $\\cos^2(\\theta) = \\frac{1 + \\cos(2\\theta)}{2}$, we get:\n$I = \\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\frac{1 + \\cos(2\\theta)}{2} d\\theta = \\left[ \\frac{\\theta}{2} + \\frac{\\sin(2\\theta)}{4} \\right]_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} = \\frac{\\pi}{4} - (-\\frac{\\pi}{4}) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nCalculating the numerical value: $\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\approx \\frac{3.14159}{2} \\approx 1.57079$.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
