    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6595",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is \\lim_{{x \\to 0}} (csc(x) - cot(x))?\n\nOptions: \nA. -1/2\nB. Undefined\nC. 1/2\nD. 1\nE. 0\nF. -1\nG. 2\nH. -2\nI. 3\nJ. Infinity\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the limit $\\lim_{x \\to 0} (\\csc(x) - \\cot(x))$, we can rewrite the trigonometric functions in terms of sine and cosine:\n$$\\csc(x) - \\cot(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sin(x)} - \\frac{\\cos(x)}{\\sin(x)} = \\frac{1 - \\cos(x)}{\\sin(x)}$$\nUsing L'Hôpital's Rule (since the form is $0/0$ as $x \\to 0$) or trigonometric identities:\n$$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{1 - \\cos(x)}{\\sin(x)} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin(x)}{\\cos(x)} = \\frac{0}{1} = 0$$\nAlternatively, using the identity $\\tan(x/2) = \\csc(x) - \\cot(x)$, we have $\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\tan(x/2) = 0$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
