    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7038",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose $f(x, y)= \\begin{{cases}}1-x-y, & x+y \\leqslant 1 \\ 0, & x+y>1\\end{{cases}}$. What is the integral of f(x,y) over the region I=[0,1]\\times[0,1]?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.33333\nB. 0.5\nC. 1.5\nD. 0\nE. 0.75\nF. 1\nG. 0.25\nH. 0.16667\nI. 0.66667\nJ. 0.83333\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the integral of $f(x, y)$ over the region $I = [0, 1] \\times [0, 1]$, we integrate the function where it is non-zero.\nThe function $f(x, y) = 1 - x - y$ is non-zero only in the region where $x + y \\le 1$ within the unit square, which is a triangle with vertices $(0,0), (1,0),$ and $(0,1)$.\n\nThe integral is:\n$$\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1-x} (1 - x - y) \\, dy \\, dx$$\nSolving the inner integral with respect to $y$:\n$$\\int_{0}^{1-x} (1 - x - y) \\, dy = \\left[ (1-x)y - \\frac{1}{2}y^2 \\right]_0^{1-x} = (1-x)^2 - \\frac{1}{2}(1-x)^2 = \\frac{1}{2}(1-x)^2$$\nSolving the outer integral with respect to $x$:\n$$\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{2}(1-x)^2 \\, dx = \\left[ -\\frac{1}{6}(1-x)^3 \\right]_0^1 = 0 - \\left( -\\frac{1}{6} \\right) = \\frac{1}{6} \\approx 0.16667$$\n\n\\boxed{H}",
