  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6713",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $X_1, X_2, \\ldots$ be a sequence of independent indetically distributed random variables drawn according to the probability mass function $p(x) = N(0,1)$. Let $q(x)=N(1,1)$ be another probability mass function. Use natural logarithm to evaluate $\\lim -\\frac{{1}}{{n}}\\log{{q(X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n)}}$ as $n \\to \\infty$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 2.1\nB. 1.4\nC. 0.5\nD. 3.5\nE. 2.8\nF. 1.8\nG. 0.7\nH. 3.1\nI. 2.5\nJ. 1.1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve for the limit $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} -\\frac{1}{n} \\log q(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)$, we recognize that since $X_i$ are i.i.d., the expression is the average of the negative log-likelihoods: $-\\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\log q(X_i)$. By the Strong Law of Large Numbers, this converges to the expected value $E_{p}[-\\log q(X)]$, which is the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence $D_{KL}(p \\| q)$ if $p$ and $q$ are normalized.\n\nGiven $p(x) = \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$ and $q(x) = \\mathcal{N}(1, 1)$, the formula for the KL divergence between two univariate Gaussians $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu_0, \\sigma_0^2)$ and $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2)$ is:\n$D_{KL}(p \\| q) = \\log \\frac{\\sigma_1}{\\sigma_0} + \\frac{\\sigma_0^2 + (\\mu_0 - \\mu_1)^2}{2\\sigma_1^2} - \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nSubstituting the values $\\mu_0=0, \\sigma_0=1, \\mu_1=1, \\sigma_1=1$:\n$D_{KL}(p \\| q) = \\log(1) + \\frac{1^2 + (0 - 1)^2}{2(1)^2} - \\frac{1}{2} = 0 + \\frac{2}{2} - 0.5 = 1 - 0.5 = 0.5$.\n\nThe limit is 0.5, which corresponds to option C.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
