  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6716",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: what is the limit of $2/\\sqrt{{\\pi}}*\\sqrt{{n}}\\int_0^1(1-x^2)^n dx$ as n goes to infinity?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1/\\sqrt{{\\pi}}\nB. 2/\\sqrt{{\\pi}}\nC. Infinity\nD. 2.0\nE. 3.0\nF. -1.0\nG. 0.5\nH. 1.5\nI. 1.0\nJ. 0.0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the limit of $2/\\sqrt{\\pi} \\cdot \\sqrt{n} \\int_0^1 (1-x^2)^n dx$ as $n \\to \\infty$, we first evaluate the integral $I_n = \\int_0^1 (1-x^2)^n dx$.\nSubstituting $x = \\sin \\theta$ or using the properties of the Beta function, we know that $I_n = \\int_0^1 (1-x^2)^n dx \\approx \\int_0^\\infty e^{-nx^2} dx$ for large $n$ (since $(1-x^2)^n \\approx e^{-nx^2}$ for $x$ near 0).\nThe Gaussian integral $\\int_0^\\infty e^{-nx^2} dx = \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{n}}$. Substituting this back into the expression:\n$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{\\pi}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2\\sqrt{n}} = 1$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
