    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the probability $P(\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} X_n / \\log n = 1)$, we use the Borel-Cantelli Lemma. Let $E_n$ be the event $\\{X_n > (1 + \\epsilon) \\log n\\}$. Since $X_n \\sim \\text{Exp}(1)$, $P(E_n) = e^{-(1+\\epsilon)\\log n} = n^{-(1+\\epsilon)}$. Because $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n^{-(1+\\epsilon)} < \\infty$ for $\\epsilon > 0$, the Borel-Cantelli Lemma implies that $X_n / \\log n \\leq 1 + \\epsilon$ almost surely for all $\\epsilon > 0$, so $\\limsup X_n / \\log n \\leq 1$ a.s. Conversely, let $F_n$ be the event $\\{X_n > (1 - \\epsilon) \\log n\\}$. Then $P(F_n) = n^{-(1-\\epsilon)}$, and since $\\sum n^{-(1-\\epsilon)} = \\infty$ for $\\epsilon > 0$, the second Borel-Cantelli Lemma implies $X_n / \\log n > 1 - \\epsilon$ infinitely often almost surely. Thus, $\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} X_n / \\log n = 1$ with probability 1.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
