      "generated_answer": "To find the limit $\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{e^{x^4/2} - \\cos(x^2)}{x^4}$, we use the Taylor series expansions around $x=0$:\n1. $e^u = 1 + u + \\frac{u^2}{2!} + \\dots$, so $e^{x^4/2} = 1 + \\frac{x^4}{2} + O(x^8)$.\n2. $\\cos(v) = 1 - \\frac{v^2}{2!} + \\dots$, so $\\cos(x^2) = 1 - \\frac{(x^2)^2}{2} + O(x^8) = 1 - \\frac{x^4}{2} + O(x^8)$.\n\nSubstituting these into the numerator:\nNumerator $= (1 + \\frac{x^4}{2}) - (1 - \\frac{x^4}{2}) + O(x^8) = \\frac{x^4}{2} + \\frac{x^4}{2} + O(x^8) = x^4 + O(x^8)$.\n\nThe limit becomes:\n$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{x^4 + O(x^8)}{x^4} = 1$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
