    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the sum $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{2}{n^2 + 4n + 3}$, we first factor the denominator:\n$n^2 + 4n + 3 = (n+1)(n+3)$.\n\nUsing partial fraction decomposition:\n$\\frac{2}{(n+1)(n+3)} = \\frac{A}{n+1} + \\frac{B}{n+3} \\implies 2 = A(n+3) + B(n+1)$.\nSetting $n=-1$ gives $2 = 2A \\implies A=1$. Setting $n=-3$ gives $2 = -2B \\implies B=-1$.\nSo, the sum is $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left( \\frac{1}{n+1} - \\frac{1}{n+3} \\right)$.\n\nThis is a telescoping series:\nSum $= (\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{4}) + (\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{6}) + (\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{7}) + \\dots$\nMost terms cancel out, leaving only the first two positive terms:\nSum $= \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{3+2}{6} = \\frac{5}{6} \\approx 0.8333$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
