      "generated_answer": "The given equation $x^2 y'' - 2y = 0$ is a Cauchy-Euler equation. We assume a solution of the form $y = x^r$, which leads to the characteristic equation $r(r-1) - 2 = 0$, or $r^2 - r - 2 = 0$.\n\nSolving for $r$, we get $(r-2)(r+1) = 0$, so $r_1 = 2$ and $r_2 = -1$. The general solution is $y(x) = C_1 x^2 + C_2 x^{-1}$. For the solution to be bounded as $x \\rightarrow 0$, the coefficient of the singular term $x^{-1}$ must be zero, meaning $C_2 = 0$. Thus, $y(x) = C_1 x^2$.\n\nApplying the initial conditions:\n$y(1) = C_1(1)^2 = 1 \\implies C_1 = 1$.\nThen $y(x) = x^2$ and $y'(x) = 2x$.\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives $y'(1) = 2(1) = 2$. Thus, $\\gamma = 2$.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
