  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7346",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $F_0(x)=log(x)$. For $n\\geq 0$ and $x>0$, let $F_{{n+1}}(x)=\\int_0^x F_n(t)dt$. Evaluate $\\lim _{{n \\rightarrow \\infty}} (n! F_n(1))/(log(n))$.\n\nOptions: \nA. π (Pi)\nB. 1.0\nC. 1/n\nD. 2.0\nE. -1.0\nF. e (Euler's number)\nG. -e (Negative Euler's number)\nH. e^-1 (Inverse of Euler's number)\nI. 0.0\nJ. Infinity\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the general form of $F_n(x)$, we use repeated integration.\nStarting with $F_0(x) = \\ln(x)$, we have:\n$F_1(x) = \\int_0^x \\ln(t) dt = x \\ln(x) - x$\n$F_2(x) = \\int_0^x (t \\ln(t) - t) dt = \\frac{x^2}{2} \\ln(x) - \\frac{x^2}{4}$\nBy induction, it can be shown that $F_n(x) = \\frac{x^n}{n!} \\left( \\ln(x) - \\sum_{k=1}^n \\frac{1}{k} \\right)$.\nEvaluating at $x=1$, we get $F_n(1) = \\frac{1}{n!} (0 - H_n) = -\\frac{H_n}{n!}$, where $H_n = \\sum_{k=1}^n \\frac{1}{k}$ is the $n$-th harmonic number.\nThe expression becomes:\n$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{n! F_n(1)}{\\ln(n)} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{-H_n}{\\ln(n)}$.\nSince $H_n \\approx \\ln(n) + \\gamma$ (where $\\gamma$ is the Euler-Mascheroni constant), the limit is:\n$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{-(\\ln(n) + \\gamma)}{\\ln(n)} = -1$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
