    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7031",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is $\\lim _{{r \\rightarrow \\infty}} (\\int_0^{{\\pi/2}} x^r sin(x) dx)/(r\\int_0^{{\\pi/2}} x^r cos(x) dx)$?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.54321\nB. 0.22222\nC. 0.77777\nD. 0.87654\nE. 0.33333\nF. 0.99999\nG. 0.63662\nH. 1.23456\nI. 0.45678\nJ. 0.11111\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the limit $\\lim_{r \\to \\infty} \\frac{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx}{r \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx}$, we use integration by parts on the numerator.\n\nLet $u = \\sin(x)$ and $dv = x^r dx$. Then $du = \\cos(x) dx$ and $v = \\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1}$.\nThe numerator becomes:\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx = \\left[ \\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1} \\sin(x) \\right]_0^{\\pi/2} - \\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1} \\cos(x) dx$\n$= \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1} - \\frac{1}{r+1} \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^{r+1} \\cos(x) dx$.\n\nNow consider the denominator $r \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx$. Using integration by parts on this integral:\nLet $u = \\cos(x)$ and $dv = x^r dx$. Then $du = -\\sin(x) dx$ and $v = \\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1}$.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx = \\left[ \\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1} \\cos(x) \\right]_0^{\\pi/2} + \\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1} \\sin(x) dx = 0 + \\frac{1}{r+1} \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^{r+1} \\sin(x) dx$.\n\nAs $r \\to \\infty$, the integral $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r f(x) dx$ is dominated by the value of $f(x)$ near $x = \\pi/2$. \nUsing Laplace's method or asymptotic expansion, $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx \\approx \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1} \\sin(\\pi/2) = \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1}$.\nSimilarly, $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx \\approx \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx$. Applying integration by parts again to the cosine integral:\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx = [x^r \\sin(x)]_0^{\\pi/2} - \\int_0^{\\pi/2} r x^{r-1} \\sin(x) dx \\approx (\\pi/2)^r - r \\frac{(\\pi/2)^r}{r} = 0$.\nWait, more accurately: $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx \\sim \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r} \\cdot 0$ is incorrect.\nThe asymptotic for $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx$ is $\\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{(r+1)^2}$ (since $\\cos(x) \\approx (\\pi/2 - x)$).\nActually, $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx \\approx \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r (\\pi/2 - x) dx = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1} - \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+2}}{r+2} \\approx \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r}$.\nThus the ratio is $\\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}/(r+1)}{r \\cdot (\\pi/2)^{r+1}/(r^2)} \\approx \\frac{1}{1} \\cdot \\frac{r^2}{r(r+1)} \\to 1$.\nHowever, checking the options, 1 is not explicitly listed, but 0.99999 is. But looking at the integration by parts again:\nNumerator $\\approx \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1}$.\nDenominator $r \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx$. Since $\\cos(x) \\approx \\pi/2 - x$ near $\\pi/2$, the integral is $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r (\\pi/2-x) dx = \\frac{\\pi/2 (\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1} - \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+2}}{r+2} = (\\pi/2)^{r+2} (\\frac{1}{r+1} - \\frac{1}{r+2}) = \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+2}}{(r+1)(r+2)}$.\nThe ratio is $\\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}/(r+1)}{r (\\pi/2)^{r+2}/((r+1)(r+2))} = \\frac{1}{r (\\pi/2) / (r+2)} \\to \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\approx 0.63662$.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
