  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7306",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If f(z) is an analytic function that maps the entire finite complex plane into the real axis, then the imaginary axis must be mapped onto\n\nOptions: \nA. a circle\nB. an infinite interval\nC. the negative real axis\nD. the positive real axis\nE. a ray\nF. a point\nG. a closed finite interval\nH. the entire real axis\nI. an open finite interval\nJ. a segment of the real axis\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we apply the Open Mapping Theorem or the Cauchy-Riemann equations. If an analytic function $f(z) = u(x, y) + iv(x, y)$ maps the entire complex plane into the real axis, then its imaginary part $v(x, y)$ is identically zero for all $z$. Since $v$ is constant, the Cauchy-Riemann equations ($\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} = 0$ and $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = -\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} = 0$) imply that $u(x, y)$ must also be constant. Therefore, $f(z)$ is a constant function, which means every point in the complex plane (including the imaginary axis) is mapped to a single point.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
