    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the Lebesgue measure of the set $E = \\{(x, y) \\in I : \\sin(x) < \\frac{1}{2}, \\cos(x+y) \\text{ is irrational}\\}$, we analyze the constraints on the unit square $I = [0, 1] \\times [0, 1]$.\n\n1.  **Constraint 1: $\\sin(x) < \\frac{1}{2}$**. For $x \\in [0, 1]$, $\\sin(x)$ is strictly increasing. $\\sin(x) = \\frac{1}{2}$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\approx 0.5236$. Thus, $\\sin(x) < \\frac{1}{2}$ holds for $x \\in [0, \\frac{\\pi}{6})$.\n2.  **Constraint 2: $\\cos(x+y)$ is irrational**. For a fixed $x$, the set of $y \\in [0, 1]$ such that $\\cos(x+y)$ is rational is a countable set (since $\\mathbb{Q}$ is countable and the function is well-behaved). The Lebesgue measure of a countable set is 0. Thus, for any $x$, the condition that $\\cos(x+y)$ is irrational holds for almost all $y \\in [0, 1]$.\n3.  **Integration**: The measure $\\mu(E)$ is the integral of the indicator function over the square:\n    $$\\mu(E) = \\int_0^{\\pi/6} \\int_0^1 1 \\, dy \\, dx = \\int_0^{\\pi/6} 1 \\, dx = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$$\n    Calculating the value: $\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\approx \\frac{3.1415926535}{6} \\approx 0.5235987756$.\n\nComparing this result to the options, Option H corresponds to this value.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
