    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7251",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose $E \\subset(0,2 \\pi) is a measurable set. \\left\\{{\\xi_n\night\\}}$ is an arbitrary sequence of real numbers. If the Lebesgue measure of E is 2, what is $\\lim _{{n \nightarrow \\infty}} \\int_E \\cos ^2 (n x+\\xi_n ) dx$? Return the numeric.\n\nOptions: \nA. $\\frac{{\\sqrt{{2}}{{2}}$\nB. 0.0\nC. $\\pi$\nD. 1.5\nE. 1.0\nF. 3.0\nG. 2.0\nH. 2.5\nI. 0.5\nJ. $\\frac{{\\pi}}{{2}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the limit $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\int_E \\cos^2(nx + \\xi_n) dx$, we use the trigonometric identity $\\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{1 + \\cos(2\\theta)}{2}$.\n\nThe integral becomes:\n$\\int_E \\cos^2(nx + \\xi_n) dx = \\int_E \\frac{1}{2} dx + \\int_E \\frac{\\cos(2nx + 2\\xi_n)}{2} dx = \\frac{1}{2} m(E) + \\frac{1}{2} \\int_E \\cos(2nx + 2\\xi_n) dx$\n\nAccording to the Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma, for any integrable function $f$ (or characteristic function of a measurable set $E$), $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\int_E \\cos(nx + \\alpha) dx = 0$. \nGiven that $m(E) = 2$, the limit is:\n$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left( \\frac{1}{2}(2) + 0 \\right) = 1$.\n\nComparing this result to the options provided, the numeric value is 1.0.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
