    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6700",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $A=\\{{n+\\sum_{{p=1}}^{{\\infty}} a_p 2^{{-2p}}: n \\in \\mathbf{{Z}}, a_p=0 or 1 \\}}$. What is the Lebesgue measure of A?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.5\nB. 0.25\nC. 4.0\nD. 0.5\nE. 2.0\nF. Infinity\nG. The measure is undefined\nH. 1.0\nI. 0.0\nJ. 3.0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the Lebesgue measure of the set $A$, we first analyze the structure of the set. The set $A$ can be written as $A = \\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}} (n + C)$, where $C = \\{ \\sum_{p=1}^{\\infty} a_p 2^{-2p} : a_p \\in \\{0, 1\\} \\}$.\n\nThe set $C$ is a Cantor-like set. Specifically, the sum is $\\sum_{p=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{a_p}{4^p}$, where each $a_p$ can be $0$ or $1$. This is a subset of $[0, \\frac{1}{3}]$ (specifically, it's a scaled version of the standard Cantor set where we only use digits 0 and 1 in base 4). The Lebesgue measure of such a set $C$ is 0, because it is a fractal formed by removing intervals at each step (similar to the Middle-Third Cantor set). \n\nSince $A$ is a countable union of sets of measure zero ($A = \\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}} (n + C)$), the total Lebesgue measure of $A$ is the sum of the measures of its components: $\\mu(A) \\le \\sum_{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}} \\mu(n + C) = \\sum_{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}} 0 = 0$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
