      "generated_answer": "To find the domain of $f(x)=\\frac{x+6}{\\sqrt{x^2-3x-4}}$, the expression inside the square root in the denominator must be strictly greater than zero:\n$x^2 - 3x - 4 > 0$.\n\nFactoring the quadratic, we get:\n$(x-4)(x+1) > 0$.\n\nThe roots are $x = 4$ and $x = -1$. Testing the intervals:\n1. For $x < -1$ (e.g., $x=-2$), $(-6)(-1) = 6 > 0$ (True).\n2. For $-1 < x < 4$ (e.g., $x=0$), $(-4)(1) = -4 > 0$ (False).\n3. For $x > 4$ (e.g., $x=5$), $(1)(6) = 6 > 0$ (True).\n\nThe domain is $(-\\infty, -1) \\cup (4, \\infty)$.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
