    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine when the map $\\phi: G \\to G$ defined by $\\phi(x) = axa^2$ is a homomorphism, we apply the homomorphism property $\\phi(xy) = \\phi(x)\\phi(y)$.\n\n1. The condition $\\phi(xy) = \\phi(x)\\phi(y)$ implies: $a(xy)a^2 = (axa^2)(aya^2)$.\n2. Simplifying the expression: $axya^2 = axa^2aya^2$.\n3. Left-canceling $a$ and right-canceling $a^2$: $xy = a^2ay = a^3y$.\n4. For this to hold for all $x, y \\in G$, we set $x=e$: $y = a^3y$, which implies $a^3 = e$.\n\nChecking the sufficiency: If $a^3 = e$, then $a^2 = a^{-1}$. The map becomes $\\phi(x) = axa^{-1}$, which is an inner automorphism (and thus a homomorphism).\n\n\\boxed{H}",
