    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine when the quotient ring $\\mathbb{Z}_3[x]/(x^3 + x^2 + c)$ is a field, the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 + x^2 + c$ must be irreducible over $\\mathbb{Z}_3$. For a cubic polynomial, irreducibility is equivalent to having no roots in $\\mathbb{Z}_3 = \\{0, 1, 2\\}$.\n\nWe test the possible values of $c \\in \\mathbb{Z}_3$ (which are $0, 1, 2$):\n- If $c = 0$, $f(0) = 0$, so it is reducible.\n- If $c = 1$, $f(0) = 1$, $f(1) = 1+1+1 = 3 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}$, so it is reducible.\n- If $c = 2$, $f(0) = 2$, $f(1) = 1+1+2 = 4 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}$, and $f(2) = 8+4+2 = 14 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3}$. Since there are no roots, $f(x)$ is irreducible when $c = 2$.\n\nComparing this to the options, $c = 2$ corresponds to choice B.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
