    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find all c in Z_3 such that Z_3[x]/(x^3 + cx^2 + 1) is a field.\n\nOptions: \nA. 4\nB. -1\nC. 0\nD. 1\nE. -3\nF. 6\nG. 5\nH. -2\nI. 2\nJ. 3\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine for which $c \\in \\mathbb{Z}_3$ the quotient ring $\\mathbb{Z}_3[x]/(x^3 + cx^2 + 1)$ is a field, the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 + cx^2 + 1$ must be irreducible over $\\mathbb{Z}_3$. Since $f(x)$ is of degree 3, it is irreducible if and only if it has no roots in $\\mathbb{Z}_3 = \\{0, 1, 2\\}$.\n\nTesting the possible values of $c \\in \\{0, 1, 2\\}$:\n1. If $c = 0$, $f(x) = x^3 + 1$. $f(2) = 8 + 1 = 9 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}$, so it is reducible.\n2. If $c = 1$, $f(x) = x^3 + x^2 + 1$. $f(0)=1, f(1)=3\\equiv 0, f(2)=8+4+1=13\\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}$. It has a root at $x=1$, so it is reducible.\n3. If $c = 2$, $f(x) = x^3 + 2x^2 + 1$. $f(0)=1, f(1)=1+2+1=4\\equiv 1, f(2)=8+2(4)+1=17\\equiv 2 \\pmod{3}$. Since there are no roots, the polynomial is irreducible.\n\nThus, the only value in $\\mathbb{Z}_3$ that makes the ring a field is $c = 2$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
