    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the number of odd terms in the expansion of $(m+n)^6$, we use the Binomial Theorem:\n$(m+n)^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^{6} \\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^k$.\n\nSince $m$ and $n$ are odd integers, any power of $m$ or $n$ is also odd. The parity of each term is therefore determined by the parity of the binomial coefficient $\\binom{6}{k}$.\nThe binomial coefficients for $n=6$ are: $\\binom{6}{0}=1, \\binom{6}{1}=6, \\binom{6}{2}=15, \\binom{6}{3}=20, \\binom{6}{4}=15, \\binom{6}{5}=6, \\binom{6}{6}=1$.\n\nThe odd coefficients are $1, 15, 15, 1$, which means there are 4 odd terms in the expansion.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
