    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7462",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Can we use the method of compass and straightedge construction to construct the edge length of a cube, such that the volume of the cube is equal to X time the volume of a given cube, where X belongs to the set {{3,17,8,27,343,1331}}? Return the answer list for the respective values of X with 1 for yes and 0 for no.\n\nOptions: \nA. [1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0]\nB. [1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0]\nC. [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1]\nD. [0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0]\nE. [0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1]\nF. [0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1]\nG. [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]\nH. [1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1]\nI. [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]\nJ. [1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine if the edge length of a cube with volume $V_{new} = X \\cdot V_{given}$ can be constructed using a compass and straightedge, we must determine if the side length $s_{new} = \\sqrt[3]{X} \\cdot s_{given}$ is constructible. A number $\\sqrt[3]{X}$ is constructible if and only if it can be expressed through a finite sequence of square roots (i.e., the degree of its minimal polynomial over $\\mathbb{Q}$ must be a power of 2).\n\nFor the given values of $X$:\n1. $X=3$: $\\sqrt[3]{3}$ is not constructible (degree 3). $\\rightarrow 0$\n2. $X=17$: $\\sqrt[3]{17}$ is not constructible (degree 3). $\\rightarrow 0$\n3. $X=8$: $\\sqrt[3]{8} = 2$ is a rational number, hence constructible. $\\rightarrow 1$\n4. $X=27$: $\\sqrt[3]{27} = 3$ is a rational number, hence constructible. $\\rightarrow 1$\n5. $X=343$: $\\sqrt[3]{343} = 7$ is a rational number, hence constructible. $\\rightarrow 1$\n6. $X=1331$: $\\sqrt[3]{1331} = 11$ is a rational number, hence constructible. $\\rightarrow 1$\n\nThe resulting list is $[0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1]$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
