  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7343",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose f is an analytic function defined on $\\{{z \\in C : IM(z) > 0\\}}$, the upper half plane. Given the information that f(f(z)) = z and f'(z) = 1/z^2 for every z. Find the most general possible expression of f(z). What is f(2)?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1\nB. -1\nC. 2\nD. 0.5\nE. -i\nF. i\nG. 4\nH. -4\nI. -0.5\nJ. -2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the function $f(z)$, we start with the given derivative $f'(z) = \\frac{1}{z^2}$. Integrating this expression gives:\n$$f(z) = \\int \\frac{1}{z^2} dz = -\\frac{1}{z} + C$$\nWe are also given the condition $f(f(z)) = z$. Substituting our expression for $f(z)$ into this condition:\n$$f(f(z)) = -\\frac{1}{f(z)} + C = -\\frac{1}{-\\frac{1}{z} + C} + C = \\frac{1}{\\frac{1 - Cz}{z}} + C = \\frac{z}{1 - Cz} + C$$\nFor $f(f(z)) = z$ to hold for all $z$, we must have:\n$z = \\frac{z}{1 - Cz} + C \\implies z - C = \\frac{z}{1 - Cz} \\implies (z - C)(1 - Cz) = z$\n$z - Cz^2 - C + C^2z = z \\implies -Cz^2 + C^2z - C = 0$\nThis must be true for all $z$, which implies $C = 0$. Thus, $f(z) = -1/z$.\nEvaluating for $f(2)$:\n$f(2) = -1/2 = -0.5$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
