  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6621",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An urn contains four balls numbered 1 through 4 . The balls are selected one at a time without replacement. A match occurs if the ball numbered $m$ is the $m$ th ball selected. Let the event $A_i$ denote a match on the $i$ th draw, $i=1,2,3,4$. Extend this exercise so that there are $n$ balls in the urn. What is the limit of this probability as $n$ increases without bound?\n\nOptions: \nA.  $1 - \\frac{{1}}{{e}}$\nB. $\\frac{{1}}{{e}}$\nC. $\\frac{{1}}{{n!}}$\nD. $\\frac{{1}}{{\\sqrt{{n}}$\nE. $\\frac{{1}}{{4}}$\nF. $\\frac{{2}}{{e}}$\nG. $\\frac{{1}}{{2}}$\nH. $e^{{-1/n}}$\nI. $\\frac{{n-1}}{{n}}$\nJ. $\\frac{{1}}{{n}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem describes a classic probability puzzle known as **derangements**. A match occurs if at least one ball $m$ is in position $m$. The event that *no* match occurs is a derangement of $n$ elements.\n\nThe probability that no match occurs (a derangement) is given by the formula $P(\\text{no match}) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\frac{(-1)^k}{k!}$. As $n \\to \\infty$, this series converges to $e^{-1} = \\frac{1}{e}$. Since the question asks for the probability of a match (the complement of no match), the limit is $1 - P(\\text{no match}) = 1 - \\frac{1}{e}$.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
