  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6819",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: suppose $lim_{{n \\rightarrow \\infty}}a_n=1$, what is the limit of (a_1+2a_2+...+na_n)/n^2?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.5\nB. 2\nC. 1/n\nD. 1\nE. 0\nF. n/(n+1)\nG. 1 + 1/n^2\nH. 2/n\nI. ln(n)\nJ. 3/2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the limit of $S_n = \\frac{\\sum_{k=1}^n k a_k}{n^2}$ as $n \\to \\infty$, we can use the Stolz-Cesàro theorem, which is a discrete analog of L'Hôpital's rule.\n\nLet $X_n = \\sum_{k=1}^n k a_k$ and $Y_n = n^2$. The limit is given by:\n$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{X_n - X_{n-1}}{Y_n - Y_{n-1}} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{n a_n}{n^2 - (n-1)^2} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{n a_n}{2n - 1}$$\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $n$:\n$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{a_n}{2 - \\frac{1}{n}} = \\frac{1}{2 - 0} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.5$$\n\n\\boxed{A}",
