    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7356",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let {{X_n: n \\geq 1}} be independent, identically distributed random variables taking integer values {{1,-1}}. Let S_0=0, S_n=\\sum_{{i=1}}^n X_i. Let P(X_i=1)=0.8 and P(X_i=-1)=0.2. The range R_n of S_0,S_1,...,S_n is the number of distinct values taken by the sequence. Then what is the limit of n^{{-1}}E[R_n] as n goes to infinity? Here E[R_n] is the expectation over the random variable R_n.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.0\nB. 0.5\nC. 1.2\nD. 1.6\nE. 0.8\nF. 0.9\nG. 1.4\nH. 0.2\nI. 0.6\nJ. 0.4\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the limit of $n^{-1}E[R_n]$ as $n \\to \\infty$, we analyze the range $R_n$ of the random walk $S_n$. The range $R_n$ is defined as the number of distinct values visited by the sequence $S_0, S_1, \\dots, S_n$.\n\nFor a random walk with a non-zero drift $\\mu = E[X_i]$, the Strong Law of Large Numbers implies that $S_n/n \\to \\mu$ almost surely. In this case, $\\mu = E[X_1] = (1)(0.8) + (-1)(0.2) = 0.6$.\nFor a random walk with drift $\\mu \\neq 0$, it is a known result in probability theory that the expected number of distinct sites visited $E[R_n]$ grows linearly with $n$, and specifically:\n$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{E[R_n]}{n} = P(\\text{the walk never returns to its starting point } S_0 = 0)$$\nFor a simple random walk on $\\mathbb{Z}$ with $p = P(X_i = 1)$ and $q = P(X_i = -1)$, the probability of ever returning to the origin is $1 - |p - q|$. Thus, the probability of never returning is $|p - q|$.\nSubstituting the given values:\n$|p - q| = |0.8 - 0.2| = 0.6$.\n\nThe limit is $0.6$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
