  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7107",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose the graph of f is both increasing and concave up on a <= x <= b. Then, using the same number of subdivisions, and with L, R, M, and T denoting, respectively, left, right, midpoint, and trapezoid sums, it follows that\n\nOptions: \nA. L <= T <= M <= R\nB. R <= M <= T <= L\nC. M <= L <= T <= R\nD. M <= R <= T <= L\nE. L <= M <= T <= R\nF. T <= L <= R <= M\nG. R <= L <= M <= T\nH. R <= T <= M <= L\nI. T <= R <= M <= L\nJ. L <= R <= T <= M\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "For a function $f$ that is both increasing and concave up on an interval $[a, b]$:\n1. Because $f$ is **increasing**, the Left sum ($L$) is an underestimation and the Right sum ($R$) is an overestimation, meaning $L < R$.\n2. Because $f$ is **concave up**, the Trapezoid sum ($T$) overestimates the area and the Midpoint sum ($M$) underestimates the area, meaning $M < T$.\n3. Specifically for concave up functions, the Midpoint sum is the most accurate underestimate and the Trapezoid sum is the most accurate overestimate relative to the endpoints, leading to the ordering $L \\le M \\le T \\le R$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
