    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | The unity of a subring must be the same as the unity of the ring. Statement 2 | Every field is an integral domain.\n\nOptions: \nA. True, True\nB. Not Enough Information, True\nC. False, True\nD. Not Enough Information, False\nE. False, False\nF. True, False\nG. True, True if the field is finite\nH. Not Enough Information, Not Enough Information\nI. True, Not Enough Information\nJ. False, Not Enough Information\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the statements:\n\n1.  **Statement 1 is False.** A subring can have a different identity element than the parent ring, or it may not have a unity at all. For example, in the ring $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}$, the subset $\\{(n, 0) : n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\}$ is a subring with unity $(1, 0)$, while the unity of the parent ring is $(1, 1)$.\n2.  **Statement 2 is True.** By definition, a field is a commutative ring with unity where every non-zero element has a multiplicative inverse; since any element $a$ such that $ab = 0$ with $a \\neq 0$ would imply $b = a^{-1}0 = 0$, it contains no zero divisors and is thus an integral domain.\n\nThe correct combination is False, True.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
