  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7440",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Given that $n > 1$, what is the smallest positive integer $n$ whose positive divisors have a product of $n^6$?\n\nOptions: \nA. 2048\nB. 30\nC. 60\nD. 120\nE. 36\nF. 72\nG. 20\nH. 12\nI. 10\nJ. 64\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the smallest positive integer $n$ whose positive divisors have a product of $n^6$, we use the formula for the product of divisors: $P(n) = n^{d(n)/2}$, where $d(n)$ is the number of divisors of $n$.\n\nGiven $P(n) = n^6$, we set $n^{d(n)/2} = n^6$, which implies $d(n)/2 = 6$, so $d(n) = 12$. We look for the smallest $n$ with exactly 12 divisors. The possible factorizations of 12 are:\n- $12$: $n = p^{11}$ (Smallest: $2^{11} = 2048$)\n- $6 \\times 2$: $n = p^5 q^1$ (Smallest: $2^5 \\cdot 3 = 96$)\n- $4 \\times 3$: $n = p^3 q^2$ (Smallest: $2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 8 \\cdot 9 = 72$)\n- $3 \\times 2 \\times 2$: $n = p^2 q^1 r^1$ (Smallest: $2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 = 4 \\cdot 15 = 60$)\n\nComparing the smallest values from each case (2048, 96, 72, 60), the smallest integer is 60.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
