    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6525",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | A homomorphism may have an empty kernel. Statement 2 | It is not possible to have a nontrivial homomorphism of some finite group into some infinite group.\n\nOptions: \nA. Statement 1 is true, Statement 2 is not given\nB. Both statements are true, but they contradict each other\nC. Both statements are not given\nD. False, True\nE. Statement 1 is not given, Statement 2 is true\nF. Statement 1 is false, Statement 2 is not given\nG. False, False\nH. True, True\nI. True, False\nJ. Statement 1 is not given, Statement 2 is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct option, we analyze the two statements:\n\n1.  **Statement 1:** A homomorphism $f: G \\to H$ must always map the identity element of $G$ to the identity element of $H$ ($f(e_G) = e_H$). Since the kernel is defined as the set of all elements in $G$ that map to $e_H$, the kernel always contains at least the identity element $e_G$. Therefore, the kernel can never be empty. **Statement 1 is False.**\n2.  **Statement 2:** A nontrivial homomorphism exists if there is a mapping where at least one element maps to something other than the identity. For example, consider the group $(\\mathbb{Z}_2, +)$ and the group $(\\mathbb{R}^*, \\times)$. A homomorphism $f: \\mathbb{Z}_2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^*$ defined by $f(0) = 1$ and $f(1) = -1$ is a nontrivial homomorphism from a finite group to an infinite group. **Statement 2 is False.**\n\nBoth statements are false.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
