  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6473",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose that $f(x)$ is a polynomial that has degree $6$ and $g(x)$ is a polynomial that has degree $3$. If $h(x)$ is also a polynomial such that $f(g(x)) + g(h(x)) + h(f(x))$ is a polynomial of degree $36$, then what is the degree of the polynomial $h$?\n\nOptions: \nA. 3\nB. 21\nC. 6\nD. 9\nE. 30\nF. 72\nG. 12\nH. 15\nI. 24\nJ. 18\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the degree of $h(x)$, let $d$ be the degree of $h$. We are given that $\\deg(f) = 6$ and $\\deg(g) = 3$.\n\nThe degrees of the three composite terms are:\n1. $\\deg(f(g(x))) = \\deg(f) \\cdot \\deg(g) = 6 \\cdot 3 = 18$.\n2. $\\deg(g(h(x))) = \\deg(g) \\cdot \\deg(h) = 3 \\cdot d$.\n3. $\\deg(h(f(x))) = \\deg(h) \\cdot \\deg(f) = d \\cdot 6$.\n\nThe degree of the sum $f(g(x)) + g(h(x)) + h(f(x))$ is the maximum of these individual degrees (unless the leading terms cancel, which is not expected here). We are told the resulting degree is 36.\nSince $18 < 36$, the maximum must come from either $3d$ or $6d$.\nComparing $3d$ and $6d$, we know $6d$ is the dominant term.\nSetting $6d = 36$, we find $d = 6$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
