  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7100",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following are valid intervals of convergence for a power series?\n(I) [−1, 3)\n(II) (−∞, 0]\n(III) [2, 2] ∪ [3, 3]\n(IV) (−∞,∞)\n\nOptions: \nA. Only (III)\nB. Only (II)\nC. (I), (III) and (IV)\nD. All of them\nE. (I) and (IV )\nF. (II) and (IV)\nG. Only (IV )\nH. (II), (III) and (IV)\nI. (I), (II) and (III)\nJ. (II) and (III)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "According to the theory of power series, the interval of convergence must be centered at some point $a$. It can take one of three forms: a single point $\\{a\\}$, a finite interval $(a-R, a+R)$ (with endpoints possibly included), or the entire real line $(-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\n1.  **(I) $[-1, 3)$**: This is a valid interval centered at $a=1$ with radius $R=2$.\n2.  **(II) $(-\\infty, 0]$**: This is invalid; if a power series converges for all $x < 0$, it must converge for all $x > 0$ as well (the interval must be symmetric around the center).\n3.  **(III) $[2, 2] \\cup [3, 3]$**: This is invalid; the set of convergence for a power series must be a single connected interval (or a single point).\n4.  **(IV) $(-\\infty, \\infty)$**: This is a valid interval (radius $R = \\infty$).\n\nThus, only (I) and (IV) are valid.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
