    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7310",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The curves y = (sin x + cos x)^4 and y = (sin x - cos x)^4 intersect at x = 0. If a is the smallest positive value of x at which these curves next intersect, determine the area between these curves from x = 0 to x = a.\n\nOptions: \nA. 5\nB. 3\nC. 2\nD. 4π\nE. 3π/2\nF. 2π\nG. π/2\nH. π\nI. 4\nJ. 1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the intersection points, we set the two equations equal:\n$(\\sin x + \\cos x)^4 = (\\sin x - \\cos x)^4$.\nTaking the square root of both sides gives $(\\sin x + \\cos x)^2 = \\pm (\\sin x - \\cos x)^2$.\nSince $1 + \\sin(2x) = 1 - \\sin(2x)$ implies $\\sin(2x) = 0$ (which gives $x = 0, \\pi/2, \\dots$) and $1 + \\sin(2x) = -(1 - \\sin(2x))$ implies $2 = 0$ (no solution), the smallest positive intersection point is $a = \\pi/2$.\n\nThe area $A$ is the integral from $0$ to $\\pi/2$ of the difference:\n$A = \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} |(1 + \\sin 2x)^2 - (1 - \\sin 2x)^2| dx = \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} |4 \\sin 2x| dx$.\n$A = \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} 4 \\sin 2x \\, dx = [-2 \\cos 2x]_{0}^{\\pi/2} = -2(\\cos \\pi - \\cos 0) = -2(-1 - 1) = 4$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
