    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | There exists a free abelian group of every positive integer rank. Statement 2 | A finitely generated abelian group is free abelian if its Betti number equals the number of elements in some generating set.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, False\nB. True, Cannot be determined\nC. False, True\nD. Cannot be determined, True\nE. False, Cannot be determined\nF. Statement 1 is false for certain positive integers, Statement 2 is false\nG. True, False\nH. Statement 1 is true only for certain positive integers, Statement 2 is true\nI. Cannot be determined, False\nJ. True, True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the statements:\n\n**Statement 1:** A free abelian group of rank $n$ is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{Z}^n$ (the direct sum of $n$ copies of the integers). This construction exists for every positive integer $n$. Thus, Statement 1 is **True**.\n\n**Statement 2:** For a finitely generated abelian group $G$, the Betti number $\\beta$ is the rank of its free part. According to the Fundamental Theorem of Finitely Generated Abelian Groups, if $G$ is generated by $k$ elements, then $\\beta \\le k$. If $\\beta = k$, the torsion subgroup must be trivial (since any torsion elements would either increase the number of necessary generators or decrease the Betti number relative to the size of the minimal generating set). Therefore, $G \\cong \\mathbb{Z}^k$, making it a free abelian group. Thus, Statement 2 is **True**.\n\nBoth statements are true.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
