    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6673",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 |  Suppose ∑|a_i| diverges and ∑ a_i = 2. There is a rearrangement a_i_k of the terms such that ∑ a_i_k = 4. Statement 2 | There exists metric spaces X and Y with X closed and bounded and a continuous mapping f : X → Y such that f(X) is NOT “closed and bounded”.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, Not Sure\nB. False, False\nC. Not Sure, True\nD. True, Not Sure\nE. Not Sure, Not Sure\nF. True, False\nG. False, True\nH. True, Cannot be determined\nI. Not Sure, False\nJ. True, True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Statement 1** is **True**. According to the Riemann Rearrangement Theorem, if a series $\\sum a_i$ is conditionally convergent (meaning $\\sum a_i$ converges but $\\sum |a_i|$ diverges), the terms can be rearranged to sum to any real number, including 4.\n\n**Statement 2** is **True**. While the image of a *compact* set under a continuous mapping is always compact (and thus closed and bounded in $\\mathbb{R}^n$), this does not hold for general metric spaces where \"closed and bounded\" does not imply compactness. For example, in an infinite-dimensional Hilbert space, one can have a closed and bounded set whose image under a continuous map is not closed or not bounded (or specifically, a continuous map from a closed bounded set in a non-compact space to another where the property fails).\n\nTherefore, both statements are true.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
