    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_8908",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose a hologram is made with light of wavelength 600 nm (orange) and then viewed with light of wavelength 500 nm (green) . How will the images differ from those that would be formed with the orange light?\n\nOptions: \nA. The reconstructed image distance = (3 / 2) the object distance\nB. the reconstructed image distance = (6 / 5) the object distance\nC. The reconstructed image distance = (4 / 5) the object distance\nD. the reconstructed image distance = 2 times the object distance\nE. the reconstructed image distance = the object distance\nF. the reconstructed image distance = (5 / 6) the object distance\nG. The reconstructed image distance = 3 times the object distance\nH. The reconstructed image distance is the same as when viewed with orange light\nI. The reconstructed image distance = (5 / 4) the object distance\nJ. The reconstructed image distance = (2 / 3) the object distance\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In holography, the holographic grating acts as a diffraction grating where the angle of diffraction $\\theta$ is proportional to the wavelength $\\lambda$ (for small angles, $\\sin \\theta \\approx \\lambda / d$). The distance to the reconstructed image $D$ is inversely proportional to the angle ($\\tan \\theta \\approx D_{object}/D_{image}$), meaning the image distance $D_{image}$ is proportional to the wavelength $\\lambda$ used for reconstruction.\n\nUsing the ratio of the wavelengths:\n$\\frac{D_{reconstructed}}{D_{object}} = \\frac{\\lambda_{viewing}}{\\lambda_{recording}} = \\frac{500\\text{ nm}}{600\\text{ nm}} = \\frac{5}{6}$.\n\nTherefore, the reconstructed image distance is $5/6$ of the original object distance.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
