  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_7785",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The emission spectrum of the doubly ionized lithium atom Li++ (Z = 3, A = 7) is identical to that of a hydrogen atom in which all the wavelengths are\n\nOptions: \nA. increased by a factor of 16\nB. increased by a factor of 25\nC. decreased by a factor of 16\nD. decreased by a factor of 25\nE. increased by a factor of 81\nF. decreased by a factor of 81\nG. increased by a factor of 49\nH. increased by a factor of 9\nI. decreased by a factor of 9\nJ. decreased by a factor of 49\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The energy levels of a hydrogen-like atom are given by $E_n = -13.6 \\text{ eV} \\frac{Z^2}{n^2}$, meaning the frequency of emitted photons ($\\nu \\propto \\Delta E$) is proportional to $Z^2$. Since the wavelength $\\lambda$ is inversely proportional to frequency ($\\lambda = c/\\nu$), the wavelength for a hydrogen-like atom is $\\lambda \\propto \\frac{1}{Z^2}$ compared to hydrogen ($Z=1$). For doubly ionized lithium ($\\text{Li}^{++}$), $Z = 3$, so the wavelengths are scaled by a factor of $\\frac{1}{3^2} = \\frac{1}{9}$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
