    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_8096",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following gives the total spin quantum number of the electrons in the ground state of neutral nitrogen (Z = 7)?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1\nB. 5/2\nC. 3\nD. 2\nE. 4 1/2\nF. 1/2\nG. 0\nH. 2 1/2\nI. 4\nJ. 3/2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the total spin quantum number ($S$) for neutral nitrogen ($Z = 7$), we look at its ground-state electron configuration: $1s^2 2s^2 2p^3$.\n\n1. The $1s$ and $2s$ orbitals are completely filled, meaning their electrons are paired and contribute 0 to the total spin.\n2. According to Hund's rule, the three electrons in the $2p$ subshell occupy separate orbitals ($2p_x, 2p_y, 2p_z$) with parallel spins to minimize electron-electron repulsion.\n3. Each unpaired electron has a spin of $s = 1/2$, so the total spin quantum number is $S = 1/2 + 1/2 + 1/2 = 3/2$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
      "success": true,
