    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_7995",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The electric field in an $x y$ plane produced by a positively charged particle is $7.2(4.0 \\hat{{\\mathrm{{i}}+3.0 \\hat{{\\mathrm{{j}}) \\mathrm{{N}} / \\mathrm{{C}}$ at the point $(3.0,3.0) \\mathrm{{cm}}$ and $100 \\hat{{\\mathrm{{i}} \\mathrm{{N}} / \\mathrm{{C}}$ at the point $(2.0,0) \\mathrm{{cm}}$. What is the $x$ coordinate of the particle?\n\nOptions: \nA. $-2.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$\nB. $6.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$\nC. $1.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$\nD. $5.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$\nE. $0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$\nF. $2.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$\nG. $4.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$\nH.  $-1.0$$\\mathrm{{~cm}}$ \nI. $3.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$\nJ. $-3.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the $x$-coordinate of the positively charged particle, we use the property that the electric field $\\vec{E}$ produced by a point charge acts along the radial line connecting the charge's position $(x_0, y_0)$ to the observation point $(x, y)$.\n\n1.  At point $P_2(2.0, 0)$, the electric field is $\\vec{E}_2 = 100 \\hat{i} \\text{ N/C}$. Since the field is purely in the $+x$ direction and the particle is positively charged, the particle must be located on the $x$-axis to the left of $P_2$. Thus, $y_0 = 0$ and $x_0 < 2.0$.\n2.  At point $P_1(3.0, 3.0)$, the electric field is $\\vec{E}_1 = 7.2(4.0 \\hat{i} + 3.0 \\hat{j}) \\text{ N/C}$, which is proportional to the vector $4.0 \\hat{i} + 3.0 \\hat{j}$.\n3.  The vector from the charge $(x_0, 0)$ to $P_1(3, 3)$ is $\\vec{r}_1 = (3-x_0)\\hat{i} + (3-0)\\hat{j}$. For the field to be in the direction $4\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j}$, the components must be proportional:\n    $\\frac{3 - x_0}{3} = \\frac{4}{3} \\implies 3 - x_0 = 4 \\implies x_0 = -1.0 \\text{ cm}$.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the particle is $-1.0 \\text{ cm}$.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
