    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_8179",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A particle of mass $m$ and velocity $u_1$ makes a head-on collision with another particle of mass $2 m$ at rest. If the coefficient of restitution is such to make the loss of total kinetic energy a maximum, what are the velocities $v_1$ after the collision?\n\nOptions: \nA. $0$\nB.   $\\frac{{1}}{{3}}$$u_1$\nC. $\\frac{{1}}{{2}}$$u_1$\nD. $\\frac{{2}}{{5}}u_1$\nE. $\\frac{{2}}{{3}}$$u_1$\nF. $\\frac{{3}}{{4}}u_1$\nG. $-\\frac{{1}}{{2}}u_1$\nH. $-\\frac{{1}}{{3}}u_1$\nI. $u_1$\nJ. $\\frac{{1}}{{4}}u_1$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the condition for the maximum loss of total kinetic energy in a head-on collision, we look at the coefficient of restitution $e$. The kinetic energy loss is maximized when the collision is perfectly inelastic ($e = 0$), meaning the two particles stick together and move with a common final velocity $v$.\n\nUsing the conservation of momentum:\n$m u_1 + 2m(0) = (m + 2m)v$\n$m u_1 = 3m v$\n$v = \\frac{1}{3}u_1$\n\nSince the particles move together after a perfectly inelastic collision, the final velocity of the first particle $v_1$ is equal to this common velocity $v$.\n\n$\\boxed{B}$",
