    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_7931",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Standing waves are produced by a 100-Hz generator in a string of fixed length. The tension in the string is increased until a new set of standing waves is produced. Will the wavelength of the new standing waves be greater than or less than the wavelength of the original standing waves?\n\nOptions: \nA. The wavelength will be greater, because the tension in the string varies inversely with the wave speed, which varies inversely with the wavelength.\nB. Less, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies directly with the wavelength.\nC. The wavelength cannot be determined without more information about the properties of the string and the wave generator.\nD. Greater, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies inversely with the wavelength.\nE. Less, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies inversely with the wavelength.\nF. Greater, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies directly with the wavelength.\nG. The wavelength will be less, because the tension in the string varies inversely with the wave speed, which varies directly with the wavelength.\nH. The wavelength will be greater, because the increased tension in the string will cause an increase in the wave speed, resulting in a longer wavelength.\nI. The wavelength will remain the same, because the tension in the string does not affect the wavelength.\nJ. The wavelength will be less, because the increased tension in the string will cause a decrease in the wave speed, resulting in a shorter wavelength.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the effect of increasing tension on the wavelength, we look at the physics of strings:\n1. **Wave Speed ($v$):** The speed of a wave on a string is given by $v = \\sqrt{T/\\mu}$, meaning the wave speed varies directly with the square root of the tension ($T$). Increasing tension increases the wave speed.\n2. **Wavelength ($\\lambda$):** The relationship between speed, frequency ($f$), and wavelength is $v = f\\lambda$. Since the generator frequency is constant (100 Hz), the wavelength $\\lambda = v/f$ varies directly with the wave speed.\n3. **Conclusion:** Increasing the tension increases the wave speed, which in turn increases the wavelength.\n\nMatching this to the options, Option F correctly states that tension varies directly with speed, and speed varies directly with wavelength.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 398,
        "output_tokens": 185,
        "total_tokens": 583
