  {
    "global index": "MathQA_360",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: n = 1 ! + 2 ! + 3 ! . . . . . + 10 ! . what is the last digit of n ^ n ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 3\nB. 4\nC. 5\nD. 6\nE. 7\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the last digit of $n^n$, we first calculate the last digit of $n = 1! + 2! + 3! + 4! + 5! + \\dots + 10!$.\n1. The last digits of the factorials are: $1! = 1$, $2! = 2$, $3! = 6$, $4! = 24 \\rightarrow 4$, and for any $k \\ge 5$, $k!$ ends in $0$.\n2. Summing these last digits: $1 + 2 + 6 + 4 + 0 + \\dots + 0 = 13$, so the last digit of $n$ is $3$.\n3. We need the last digit of $3^n$; since $n$ ends in $3$, it is an odd number not divisible by 4 (specifically, $n \\equiv 3 \\pmod 4$ because $1!+2!+3!+4! = 33$), and the powers of 3 follow the cycle $\\{3, 9, 7, 1\\}$, resulting in $3^3$ ending in 7.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 121,
        "output_tokens": 263,
