    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The real line under the $K$-topology is a classic example of a space that is **Hausdorff** ($T_2$) but fails to be regular or normal because the set $K = \\{1/n : n \\in \\mathbb{Z}_+\\}$ is closed but cannot be separated from the point $0$ by disjoint open sets. Despite these failures, it remains a refinement of the standard Euclidean topology, meaning it inherits the property of being a $T_2$ space. The specific property often highlighted in this context, which it shares with the standard real line but fails to maintain regularity, is being **second-countable**.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{second-countable}}",
