    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The latching current of SCR is 20 mA. Its holding current will be\n\nOptions: \nA. 5 mA.\nB. 23 mA.\nC. 25 mA.\nD. 20 mA.\nE. 30 mA.\nF. 40 mA.\nG. 50 mA.\nH. 10 mA.\nI. 60 mA.\nJ. 15 mA.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In a Silicon Controlled Rectifier (SCR), the **latching current** ($I_L$) is the minimum anode current required to maintain the device in the ON state immediately after the gate trigger is removed. The **holding current** ($I_H$) is the minimum anode current below which the device returns to the OFF state. It is a fundamental characteristic of SCRs that the latching current is always greater than the holding current ($I_L > I_H$).\n\nGiven $I_L = 20\\text{ mA}$, the holding current must be less than $20\\text{ mA}$. Among the options provided, the most typical relationship is that $I_H$ is roughly half to two-thirds of $I_L$, making $10\\text{ mA}$ (or any value $< 20\\text{ mA}$) a possible answer, but $10\\text{ mA}$ is a standard textbook example for a $20\\text{ mA}$ latching current.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
