    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the cosine series for $f(x)$ on $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi$, we calculate the coefficients $a_n$:\n1. $a_0 = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} f(x) dx = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi} 1 dx = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{2} = 1$. The constant term in the series is $\\frac{a_0}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n2. $a_n = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi} \\cos(nx) dx = \\frac{2}{n\\pi} [\\sin(nx)]_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi} = -\\frac{2}{n\\pi} \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n3. If $n$ is even ($n=2k$), $\\sin(k\\pi) = 0$. If $n$ is odd ($n=2k+1$), $\\sin\\left(\\frac{(2k+1)\\pi}{2}\\right) = (-1)^k$.\n4. Thus, $a_{2k+1} = -\\frac{2}{(2k+1)\\pi} (-1)^k = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{2k+1}$.\n\nSubstituting these into the series $f(x) = \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n \\cos(nx)$, we get:\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{2} + \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{2k+1} \\cos((2k+1)x) = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^k \\cos((2k+1)x)}{2k+1}$.\n\nLooking at the options, Option F matches this form (despite the slightly non-standard notation $\\infty \\sum$ instead of $\\sum^\\infty$).\n\n\\boxed{F}",
