    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the cumulative distribution function (CDF) $F(x, y, z)$, we integrate the joint density function $f(u, v, w) = e^{-(u+v+w)}$ over the region $0 < u < x$, $0 < v < y$, and $0 < w < z$:\n\n$$F(x, y, z) = \\int_{0}^{z} \\int_{0}^{y} \\int_{0}^{x} e^{-(u+v+w)} \\, du \\, dv \\, dw$$\n\nSince the variables are independent (the joint density is the product of $e^{-u}$, $e^{-v}$, and $e^{-w}$), the integral separates into the product of three identical integrals:\n$$F(x, y, z) = \\left( \\int_{0}^{x} e^{-u} \\, du \\right) \\left( \\int_{0}^{y} e^{-v} \\, dv \\right) \\left( \\int_{0}^{z} e^{-w} \\, dw \\right)$$\n$$\\int_{0}^{x} e^{-u} \\, du = [-e^{-u}]_0^x = 1 - e^{-x}$$\nThus, $F(x, y, z) = (1 - e^{-x})(1 - e^{-y})(1 - e^{-z})$.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
