    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10350",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A single phase one pulse controlled circuit has a resistance R and counter emf E load 400 sin(314 t) as the source voltage. For a load counter emf of 200 V, the range of firing angle control is\n\nOptions: \nA. 60° to 180°.\nB. 90° to 150°.\nC. 60° to 150°.\nD. 30° to 120°.\nE. 30° to 180°.\nF. 30° to 150°.\nG. 60° to 120°.\nH. 90° to 180°.\nI. 45° to 180°.\nJ. 45° to 135°.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the range of the firing angle $\\alpha$, we analyze the conditions for the thyristor to conduct in a single-phase one-pulse controlled circuit.\n\n1.  **Minimum Firing Angle ($\\alpha_{min}$):** The thyristor cannot conduct until the source voltage $v(t)$ exceeds the counter emf $E$. The source voltage is $v(t) = V_m \\sin(\\omega t)$, where $V_m = 400\\text{ V}$ and $E = 200\\text{ V}$.\n    Setting $400 \\sin(\\alpha_{min}) = 200 \\implies \\sin(\\alpha_{min}) = 0.5 \\implies \\alpha_{min} = 30^\\circ$.\n\n2.  **Maximum Firing Angle ($\\alpha_{max}$):** The thyristor will stop conducting when the source voltage falls below the counter emf $E$ during the negative half-cycle or when the voltage reaches the point where $v(t) = E$.\n    $400 \\sin(\\alpha_{max}) = 200 \\implies \\alpha_{max} = 180^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 150^\\circ$.\n\nThus, the range of the firing angle control is $30^\\circ$ to $150^\\circ$.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
