    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the Fourier cosine series over the interval 0 < x < c for the function f(x) = x.\n\nOptions: \nA. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c + (2c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_k=1 [{{cos[(2k)\\pix/ c]}} / (2k)^2]\nB. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c + (4c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_k=0 [{{cos[(2k + 1)\\pix/ c]}} / (2k + 1)^2]\nC. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c - (4c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_n=1 [1 / n^2]cos{{(n\\pix) / c}}\nD. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c - (4c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_k=0 [{{cos[(2k + 1)\\pix/ c]}} / (2k + 1)^2]\nE. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c - (4c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_k=1 [{{cos[(2k)\\pix/ c]}} / (2k)^2]\nF. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c - (2c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_n=0 [{{1 - (-1)^n}} / n^2]cos{{(n\\pix) / c}}\nG. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c + (4c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_n=1 [{{(-1)^n}} / n^2]cos{{(n\\pix) / c}}\nH. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c + (2c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_n=1 [{{1 - (- 1)^n}} / n^2]cos{{(n\\pix) / c}}\nI. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c + (2c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_n=1 [(-1)^n / n^2]cos{{(n\\pix) / c}}\nJ. f(x) \\sim (1 / 2)c - (2c / \\pi^2)\\infty\\sum_n=1 [{{1 - (- 1)^n}} / n^2]cos{{(n\\pix) / c}}\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the Fourier cosine series for $f(x) = x$ on $0 < x < c$, we use the formula:\n$f(x) = \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right)$\n\n1.  **Calculate $a_0$**:\n    $a_0 = \\frac{2}{c} \\int_0^c x \\, dx = \\frac{2}{c} \\left[ \\frac{x^2}{2} \\right]_0^c = \\frac{2}{c} \\cdot \\frac{c^2}{2} = c$.\n    Thus, $\\frac{a_0}{2} = \\frac{c}{2}$.\n\n2.  **Calculate $a_n$**:\n    $a_n = \\frac{2}{c} \\int_0^c x \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right) \\, dx$.\n    Using integration by parts: $u = x, dv = \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right)dx \\implies du = dx, v = \\frac{c}{n\\pi} \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right)$.\n    $a_n = \\frac{2}{c} \\left[ \\frac{cx}{n\\pi} \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right) \\Big|_0^c - \\int_0^c \\frac{c}{n\\pi} \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right) \\, dx \\right]$\n    $a_n = \\frac{2}{c} \\left[ 0 - \\frac{c}{n\\pi} \\left( -\\frac{c}{n\\pi} \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right) \\right) \\Big|_0^c \\right] = \\frac{2}{c} \\cdot \\frac{c^2}{n^2\\pi^2} [\\cos(n\\pi) - 1] = \\frac{2c}{n^2\\pi^2} ((-1)^n - 1)$.\n\n3.  **Analyze $a_n$**:\n    If $n$ is even, $a_n = 0$.\n    If $n$ is odd, $a_n = \\frac{2c}{n^2\\pi^2} (-2) = -\\frac{4c}{n^2\\pi^2}$.\n    Substituting $n = 2k + 1$ for $k=0, 1, 2, \\dots$:\n    $f(x) = \\frac{c}{2} - \\frac{4c}{\\pi^2} \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\cos((2k+1)\\pi x/c)}{(2k+1)^2}$.\n\nThis matches Option D.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
