    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To convert the complex Fourier series $f(t) = \\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} c_k e^{jkt}$ to trigonometric form, we use the coefficient $c_k = \\frac{1}{1+jk}$.\n\n1.  **Find $c_0$**: For $k=0$, $c_0 = \\frac{1}{1+0} = 1$.\n2.  **Analyze $c_k$ for $k > 0$**: \n    $c_k = \\frac{1}{1+jk} \\cdot \\frac{1-jk}{1-jk} = \\frac{1-jk}{1+k^2}$.\n3.  **Apply the conversion formula**: $f(t) = c_0 + \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} (c_k e^{jkt} + c_{-k} e^{-jkt}) = c_0 + \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} 2\\text{Re}(c_k e^{jkt})$.\n    $\\text{Re}(c_k e^{jkt}) = \\text{Re}\\left( \\frac{1-jk}{1+k^2} (\\cos kt + j\\sin kt) \\right) = \\frac{1}{1+k^2} (\\cos kt + k\\sin kt)$.\n4.  **Combine**: $f(t) = 1 + 2\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\cos kt + k\\sin kt}{1+k^2}$.\n\nComparing this result to the options, it matches Option D.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
