      "generated_answer": "To solve the inverse Laplace transforms:\n\n(a) For $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{2s}{(s^2+1)^2}\\right\\}$, we use the property $\\mathcal{L}\\{t \\sin(at)\\} = \\frac{2as}{(s^2+a^2)^2}$. With $a=1$, the result is $t \\sin t$.\n(b) For $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{s}}\\right\\}$, we use the general formula $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{s^{-n}\\right\\} = \\frac{t^{n-1}}{\\Gamma(n)}$. With $n = \\frac{1}{2}$, the result is $\\frac{t^{-1/2}}{\\Gamma(1/2)} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi t}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}$.\n\nMatching these results to the options, we find that Option A corresponds to $t \\sin t$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}$.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
