    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the sampling time $T_s$ and the maximum pulse duration $\\tau_{max}$, we perform the following calculations:\n\n1.  **Sampling Rate ($f_s$):** Each of the $N = 2000$ signals has a bandwidth $B = 5\\text{ kHz}$. At the Nyquist rate, each signal must be sampled at $2B = 10\\text{ kHz}$. For $N$ signals time-division multiplexed, the aggregate sampling rate is $f_s = N \\times 2B = 2000 \\times 10,000 = 2 \\times 10^7\\text{ Hz}$.\n2.  **Sampling Time ($T_s$):** The time interval between the start of each sample is $T_s = 1/f_s = 1 / (2 \\times 10^7) = 0.5 \\times 10^{-7}\\text{ sec}$ (Wait, checking the options provided, there is a mismatch in scale). \n3.  **Re-evaluating based on Options:** If the question implies $T_s$ is the sampling interval *per individual signal*, then $T_s = 1/(2B) = 1/10,000 = 1 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ sec}$.\n4.  **Pulse Duration ($\\tau_{max}$):** In TDM, the total time available for one cycle is $T_s$ (per signal). The time allocated for one pulse among $N$ signals is $T_{pulse} = T_s / N = (1 \\times 10^{-4}) / 2000 = 0.5 \\times 10^{-7}\\text{ sec}$. To avoid overlap, $\\tau_{max}$ must be less than or equal to this slot.\n\nComparing these results ($T_s = 1 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ sec}$ and $\\tau_{max} = 0.5 \\times 10^{-7}\\text{ sec}$) to the options, they match Option E.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
