    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10271",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An SCR has half cycle surge current rating of 3000 A for 50 Hz supply. One cycle surge current will be\n\nOptions: \nA. 2121.32 A.\nB. 6000 A.\nC. 2000 A.\nD. 7000 A.\nE. 5000 A.\nF. 3500 A.\nG. 1500 A.\nH. 4242.64 A.\nI. 3000 A.\nJ. 2500 A.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The surge current rating of an SCR is typically based on the $I^2t$ value (thermal energy), which remains constant regardless of the duration of the surge. For a sinusoidal current, the relationship between the half-cycle surge current ($I_{h}$) and the full-cycle surge current ($I_{f}$) is governed by the formula $I_{f} = \\frac{I_{h}}{\\sqrt{2}}$ if the energy is distributed over a full cycle, but in standard power electronics rating conventions for surge currents, the full-cycle rating is often calculated as $I_{f} = I_{h} \\times \\sqrt{2}$ (or $3000 \\times 1.414$) to maintain the same $I^2t$ limit across a different time interval.\n\nCalculation: $3000 \\times \\sqrt{2} \\approx 3000 \\times 1.4142 = 4242.64 \\text{ A}$.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
