    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the capacitance $C$ of a parallel-plate capacitor with a varying relative permittivity $\\epsilon_r(x)$, we treat the capacitor as an infinite series of infinitesimal capacitors in series. The formula for total capacitance is:\n$$\\frac{1}{C} = \\int_{0}^{d} \\frac{dx}{\\epsilon_0 \\epsilon_r(x) A}$$\nGiven $\\epsilon_r(x) = \\frac{1}{1 - x^2/(3d^2)}$, the integrand becomes:\n$$\\frac{1}{C} = \\frac{1}{\\epsilon_0 A} \\int_{0}^{d} \\left(1 - \\frac{x^2}{3d^2}\\right) dx = \\frac{1}{\\epsilon_0 A} \\left[ x - \\frac{x^3}{9d^2} \\right]_{0}^{d} = \\frac{1}{\\epsilon_0 A} \\left( d - \\frac{d}{9} \\right) = \\frac{8d}{9\\epsilon_0 A}$$\nSolving for $C$:\n$$C = \\frac{9\\epsilon_0 A}{8d}$$\nMatching this result with the options (where $\\epsilon_v$ likely denotes $\\epsilon_0$ and $a$ denotes area $A$), we find Option I.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
