    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10901",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Determine the scalar potential function of the vector function A^\\ding{{217}}: A^\\ding{{217}} =2xy\\^{{\\i}}+ x^2\\^{{\\j}} + 3z^2k̂\n\nOptions: \nA. 2x^2y + 3z^3 + C\nB. x^2y + z^3 + C\nC. xy^2 + z^3 + C\nD. 2xy + z^3 + C\nE. x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + C\nF. 2xy + x^2 + 3z^2 + C\nG. 2xy^2 + z^2 + C\nH. x^2y + 3z^2 + C\nI. x^2y^2 + z^3 + C\nJ. x^2y + z^2 + C\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the scalar potential function $\\phi$ for the vector field $\\mathbf{A} = 2xy\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\mathbf{j} + 3z^2\\mathbf{k}$, we integrate each component with respect to its corresponding variable:\n\n1.  $\\int 2xy \\, dx = x^2y + f(y, z)$\n2.  $\\int x^2 \\, dy = x^2y + g(x, z)$\n3.  $\\int 3z^2 \\, dz = z^3 + h(x, y)$\n\nCombining these results, the general scalar potential function is $\\phi = x^2y + z^3 + C$. Comparing this to the given options, it matches Option B.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
