    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10772",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let F= kr^nr̂ Evaluate \\int\\int_sF\\bullet n̂dsand \\int\\int_sF× n̂ds wheres is a sphere of radius a centered at the origin.\n\nOptions: \nA. 2\\pi k a^n+3, -1\nB. 4\\pi k a^n+2, 0\nC. 2\\pi k a^n+2, 0\nD. 6\\pi k a^n+2, \\pi\nE. 3\\pi k a^n+1, \\pi a^n\nF. 4\\pi k a^n+3, 1\nG. 4\\pi k a^n, 0\nH. 4\\pi k a^n-1, 0\nI. 2\\pi k a^n, 2\\pi a^n\nJ. 4\\pi k a^n+1, 0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the given integrals for the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = kr^n\\mathbf{\\hat{r}}$ over a sphere $S$ of radius $a$:\n\n1.  **First Integral ($\\iint_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{\\hat{n}} \\, dS$):** On the surface of the sphere, the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{\\hat{n}}$ is equal to the radial unit vector $\\mathbf{\\hat{r}}$. Thus, $\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{\\hat{n}} = (ka^n\\mathbf{\\hat{r}}) \\cdot \\mathbf{\\hat{r}} = ka^n$. The surface area of the sphere is $4\\pi a^2$.\n    $$\\iint_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{\\hat{n}} \\, dS = \\int_0^\\pi \\int_0^{2\\pi} (ka^n) a^2 \\sin\\theta \\, d\\phi \\, d\\theta = ka^n(4\\pi a^2) = 4\\pi k a^{n+2}$$\n\n2.  **Second Integral ($\\iint_S \\mathbf{F} \\times \\mathbf{\\hat{n}} \\, dS$):** Since $\\mathbf{F}$ is in the direction of $\\mathbf{\\hat{r}}$ and $\\mathbf{\\hat{n}} = \\mathbf{\\hat{r}}$, the cross product $\\mathbf{F} \\times \\mathbf{\\hat{n}} = (ka^n\\mathbf{\\hat{r}}) \\times \\mathbf{\\hat{r}} = 0$.\n    $$\\iint_S \\mathbf{F} \\times \\mathbf{\\hat{n}} \\, dS = \\iint_S 0 \\, dS = 0$$\n\nComparing these results ($4\\pi k a^{n+2}$ and $0$) to the options provided, Option B matches these values.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
