    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10912",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find y [n] such that y [n] - (5/6)y [n - 1] + (1/6)y [n - 2] = 3^n and y [- 1] = y [- 2] = 0, using the sum of the steady-state and homogene-ous solutions.\n\nOptions: \nA. y [n] = (26/20) 3^n - (3/5) (1/2)^n + (1/4) (1/3)^n\nB. y [n] = (27/20) 2^n - (3/5) (1/2)^n + (1/4) (1/3)^n\nC. y [n] = (27/20) 3^n - (3/5) (1/2)^n + (1/4) (1/3)^n.\nD. y [n] = (27/20) 3^n - (3/4) (1/2)^n + (1/4) (1/3)^n\nE. y [n] = (27/20) 3^n - (2/5) (1/2)^n + (1/4) (1/3)^n.\nF. y [n] = (27/20) 3^n + (3/5) (1/2)^n + (1/4) (1/3)^n\nG. y [n] = (27/20) 3^n - (3/5) (1/2)^n - (1/4) (1/3)^n\nH. y [n] = (27/20) 3^n - (4/5) (1/2)^n + (1/4) (1/3)^n.\nI. y [n] = (27/20) 3^n - (1/5) (1/2)^n + (1/4) (1/3)^n.\nJ. y [n] = (27/19) 3^n - (3/5) (1/2)^n + (1/4) (1/3)^n\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the linear difference equation $y[n] - \\frac{5}{6}y[n-1] + \\frac{1}{6}y[n-2] = 3^n$:\n\n1.  **Homogeneous Solution ($y_h[n]$):** The characteristic equation is $r^2 - \\frac{5}{6}r + \\frac{1}{6} = 0$. Solving for $r$ using the quadratic formula gives $r = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $r = \\frac{1}{3}$. Thus, $y_h[n] = A(\\frac{1}{2})^n + B(\\frac{1}{3})^n$.\n2.  **Particular Solution ($y_p[n]$):** Assume $y_p[n] = C(3^n)$. Substituting into the equation: $C(3^n) - \\frac{5}{6}C(3^{n-1}) + \\frac{1}{6}C(3^{n-2}) = 3^n$.\n    Dividing by $3^{n-2}$: $9C - \\frac{15}{6}C + \\frac{1}{6}C = 9 \\implies C(9 - 2.5 + 0.166...) = 9 \\implies C(\\frac{54-15+1}{6}) = 9 \\implies C(\\frac{40}{6}) = 9 \\implies C = \\frac{54}{40} = \\frac{27}{20}$.\n3.  **General Solution:** $y[n] = \\frac{27}{20}3^n + A(\\frac{1}{2})^n + B(\\frac{1}{3})^n$. Using initial conditions $y[-1] = 0$ and $y[-2] = 0$:\n    - For $n=-1$: $\\frac{27}{20} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + 2A + 3B = 0 \\implies 2A + 3B = -\\frac{9}{20}$\n    - For $n=-2$: $\\frac{27}{20} \\cdot \\frac{1}{9} + 4A + 9B = 0 \\implies 4A + 9B = -\\frac{3}{20}$\n    Multiplying the first eq by 2: $4A + 6B = -\\frac{18}{20}$. Subtracting this from the second eq: $3B = \\frac{15}{20} \\implies B = \\frac{5}{20} = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n    Substituting $B$ back: $2A + \\frac{3}{4} = -\\frac{9}{20} \\implies 2A = -\\frac{9}{20} - \\frac{15}{20} = -\\frac{24}{20} = -\\frac{6}{5} \\implies A = -\\frac{3}{5}$.\n\nThe final solution is $y[n] = \\frac{27}{20}3^n - \\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{1}{2})^n + \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{3})^n$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
