    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10034",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A Gaussian filter with \\vertH(\\omega)\\vert^2 = e^-(\\omega)2 is used to filter noise at the output of an amplifier stage. Determine the bandwidth of the filter.\n\nOptions: \nA. (1 / 3\\surd\\pi)\nB. (3 / 4√π)\nC. (1 / 6√π)\nD. (2 / √π)\nE. (1 / 4\\surd\\pi)\nF. (1 / 2\\surd\\pi)\nG. (1 / 8√π)\nH. (1 / √π)\nI. (1 / 5\\surd\\pi)\nJ. (1 / 7√π)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the bandwidth of a Gaussian filter, we typically refer to the noise equivalent bandwidth (NEB) for a filter with a power spectral density $|H(\\omega)|^2$.\n\n1. The noise bandwidth $B$ is defined as the integral of the power response divided by the peak value:\n   $B = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} |H(\\omega)|^2 d\\omega$ (for a normalized peak of 1).\n2. Given $|H(\\omega)|^2 = e^{-\\omega^2}$, we use the standard Gaussian integral $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} e^{-ax^2} dx = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{a}}$.\n3. Here $a=1$, so the integral is $\\sqrt{\\pi}$. Thus, $B = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\pi} = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\pi}}$.\n\nComparing this result to the given options, it matches Option F.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
      "success": true,
