    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10857",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The forces experienced by a test charge q for three different velocities at a point in a region characterized by electric and magnetic fields are given by F_1= q[E_0i_x + (E_0 - v_0B_0)i_y]forv_1= v_0i_x F_2= q[(E_0 + v_0B_0)i_x + E_0i_y]forv_2= v_0i_y- F_3= q[E_0i_x + E_0i_y]forv_3= v_0i_z where v_0, E_0, and B_0 are constants. FindE and B at the point.\n\nOptions: \nA. E= E_0 (i_x +i_y), B= B_0i_z\nB. E= E_0(i_y - i_x), B= -B_0(i_y + i_z)\nC. E= E_0(i_x + 2i_y), B= B_0(i_y - i_z)\nD. E= -E_0 (i_x +i_y), B= B_0i_z\nE. E= 0, B= B_0(i_x + i_y + i_z)\nF. E= E_0 (i_x +i_y), B= -B_0i_z\nG. E= E_0 (i_x - i_y), B= B_0i_z\nH. E= E_0i_x, B= B_0(i_x + i_y)\nI. E= 2E_0(i_x + i_y), B= 0.5B_0i_z\nJ. E= -E_0i_y, B= B_0(i_x - i_z)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The force on a charge $q$ is given by the Lorentz force formula: $\\vec{F} = q(\\vec{E} + \\vec{v} \\times \\vec{B})$.\n\n1. For $\\vec{v}_3 = v_0 \\hat{k}$, $\\vec{F}_3 = q(\\vec{E} + v_0 \\hat{k} \\times \\vec{B}) = q(E_0 \\hat{i} + E_0 \\hat{j})$. If we assume $\\vec{B}$ has no $z$-component (as suggested by options A, D, F, G), the term $v_0 \\hat{k} \\times \\vec{B}$ would involve $x$ and $y$ components of $\\vec{B}$. If $\\vec{B} = B_0 \\hat{k}$, then $\\vec{v}_3 \\times \\vec{B} = 0$, implying $\\vec{E} = E_0(\\hat{i} + \\hat{j})$.\n2. Testing $\\vec{E} = E_0(\\hat{i} + \\hat{j})$ and $\\vec{B} = B_0 \\hat{k}$ with $\\vec{v}_1 = v_0 \\hat{i}$:\n   $\\vec{F}_1 = q[E_0(\\hat{i} + \\hat{j}) + (v_0 \\hat{i} \\times B_0 \\hat{k})] = q[E_0 \\hat{i} + E_0 \\hat{j} - v_0 B_0 \\hat{j}] = q[E_0 \\hat{i} + (E_0 - v_0 B_0) \\hat{j}]$. This matches the given $\\vec{F}_1$.\n3. Testing with $\\vec{v}_2 = v_0 \\hat{j}$:\n   $\\vec{F}_2 = q[E_0(\\hat{i} + \\hat{j}) + (v_0 \\hat{j} \\times B_0 \\hat{k})] = q[E_0 \\hat{i} + E_0 \\hat{j} + v_0 B_0 \\hat{i}] = q[(E_0 + v_0 B_0) \\hat{i} + E_0 \\hat{j}]$. This matches the given $\\vec{F}_2$.\n\nThus, $\\vec{E} = E_0(\\hat{i} + \\hat{j})$ and $\\vec{B} = B_0 \\hat{k}$.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
