    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10434",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Four identical alternators each are rated for 20 MVA, 11 KV having a subtransient reactance of 16% are working in parallel. The short circuit level at the busbar is\n\nOptions: \nA. 600 MVA\nB. 800 MVA\nC. 125 MVA\nD. 500 MVA\nE. 700 MVA\nF. 300 MVA\nG. 200 MVA\nH. 400 MVA\nI. 900 MVA\nJ. 100 MVA\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the short circuit level of the busbar, we first determine the equivalent subtransient reactance of the four identical alternators connected in parallel.\n\n1.  **Equivalent Reactance ($X_{eq}$):** Since four identical alternators are in parallel, the equivalent reactance is $X_{eq} = \\frac{X}{n} = \\frac{16\\%}{4} = 4\\%$ (or 0.04 per unit).\n2.  **Total MVA Base ($S_{total}$):** The total rating of the four alternators is $4 \\times 20\\text{ MVA} = 80\\text{ MVA}$.\n3.  **Short Circuit Level (SCL):** The short circuit level is calculated as $\\text{SCL} = \\frac{S_{base}}{X_{eq(pu)}}$. Using the rating of one machine as the base ($20\\text{ MVA}$) and the equivalent reactance relative to that base ($4\\%$):\n    $\\text{SCL} = \\frac{20\\text{ MVA}}{0.04} \\times 4$ is not the correct approach; rather, $\\text{SCL} = \\frac{\\text{Total MVA}}{\\text{Equivalent Reactance}} = \\frac{80\\text{ MVA}}{0.04}$ is also incorrect because the $4\\%$ is relative to the $20\\text{ MVA}$ base.\n    The correct calculation is: $\\text{SCL} = \\frac{\\text{MVA rating of one machine}}{X_{pu}} \\times n = \\frac{20}{0.16} \\times 4 = 125 \\times 4 = 500\\text{ MVA}$.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
