  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10472",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let A= kr^nr̂(in spherical coordinates) Evaluate \\int\\int\\int_v \\nabla \\bulletAdv wherev is a sphere of radius a with center at the origin.\n\nOptions: \nA. 4\\pi k a^n+2\nB. \\pi k a^n+2\nC. 2\\pi k a^n+1\nD. 5\\pi k a^n+3\nE. 6\\pi k a^n+1\nF. 4\\pi k a^n+1\nG. 2\\pi k a^n+2\nH. 4\\pi k a^(n-1)\nI. 4\\pi k a^n\nJ. 3\\pi k a^n\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the volume integral $\\iiint_V \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{A} \\, dV$, we can use the Divergence Theorem, which states that the volume integral of the divergence is equal to the flux through the bounding surface $S$:\n$$\\iiint_V \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{A} \\, dV = \\iint_S \\mathbf{A} \\cdot d\\mathbf{a}$$\nFor a sphere of radius $a$ centered at the origin, the surface element is $d\\mathbf{a} = a^2 \\sin\\theta \\, d\\theta \\, d\\phi \\, \\hat{r}$. Substituting $\\mathbf{A} = kr^n \\hat{r}$ at the surface ($r=a$):\n$$\\iint_S (ka^n \\hat{r}) \\cdot (a^2 \\sin\\theta \\, d\\theta \\, d\\phi \\, \\hat{r}) = ka^{n+2} \\int_0^\\pi \\sin\\theta \\, d\\theta \\int_0^{2\\pi} d\\phi = ka^{n+2} (2)(2\\pi) = 4\\pi k a^{n+2}$$\nComparing this result to the provided options, Option A represents $4\\pi k a^{n+2}$.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
