    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the maximum effective aperture ($A_{em}$) of an antenna, we use the relationship between the radiation resistance ($R_r$) and the maximum gain ($G_{max}$):\n$A_{em} = \\frac{\\lambda^2}{4\\pi} G_{max}$.\n\nFor a short dipole (Hertzian dipole), the radiation resistance is given as $R_r = \\frac{\\eta}{6\\pi} (\\beta l)^2$, where $\\eta = \\sqrt{\\mu_0/\\epsilon_0} \\approx 120\\pi$. The maximum directivity (gain) of a short dipole is known to be $1.5$. \n\nPlugging the gain into the formula:\n$A_{em} = \\frac{\\lambda^2}{4\\pi} \\times 1.5 = \\frac{1.5}{12.566} \\lambda^2 \\approx 0.11936 \\lambda^2$.\n\nComparing this result to the given options, it matches option F.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
