    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10608",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If the speed of an airplane is measured by means of a stagnation tube, what correction must be applied to the constant-density indication when the plane is cruising at 300 mph?\n\nOptions: \nA. - 10 mph\nB. 25 mph\nC. -30 mph\nD. -15 mph\nE. - 5 mph\nF. 10 mph\nG. 5 mph\nH. 0 mph\nI. 15 mph\nJ. -20 mph\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the correction for a stagnation tube (Pitot tube) measurement at 300 mph, we must account for the **compressibility of air**. At speeds around 300 mph (approximately Mach 0.4), air can no longer be treated as an incompressible fluid (constant density), and the measured pressure indicates a speed higher than the actual airspeed.\n\nThe compressibility correction for airspeed is typically estimated using the formula $V_{actual} \\approx V_{indicated} (1 - \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2} M^2)$, or by referring to standard compressibility correction tables. For an indicated speed of 300 mph, the correction is approximately $-5\\%$ to $-7\\%$, which aligns with a correction of roughly $-15$ to $-20$ mph. Based on standard textbook problems of this type in aerodynamics, the commonly accepted correction for this specific speed is $-15$ mph.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
