    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10159",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The intensity of solar radiation at a point in space that is at the mean distance of the earth from the sun is 1.35 × 10^3 J \\bullet m^-2 \\bullet sec^-1. If radiation from the sun were all at one wavelength, what would be the amplitude of electromagnetic waves from the sun at the position of the earth?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.01 × 10^3 V \bullet m^-1 and 3.70 × 10^-6Wb\bullet m^-2\nB. 1.00 × 10^3 V \bullet m^-1 and 3.00 × 10^-6Wb\bullet m^-2\nC. 1.01 × 10^3 V \bullet m^-1 and 3.37 × 10^-6Wb\bullet m^-2\nD. 1.11 × 10^3 V \bullet m^-1 and 4.37 × 10^-6Wb\bullet m^-2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the amplitude of the electromagnetic waves, we use the formula for intensity $I = \\frac{1}{2} c \\epsilon_0 E_0^2$. \n\n1.  **Calculate $E_0$**: Using $I = 1.35 \\times 10^3 \\text{ W/m}^2$, $c \\approx 3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m/s}$, and $\\epsilon_0 \\approx 8.854 \\times 10^{-12} \\text{ F/m}$, we get $E_0 = \\sqrt{\\frac{2I}{c\\epsilon_0}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 1350}{(3 \\times 10^8)(8.854 \\times 10^{-12})}} \\approx 1008.5 \\text{ V/m} \\approx 1.01 \\times 10^3 \\text{ V/m}$.\n2.  **Calculate $B_0$**: Using $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c} = \\frac{1008.5}{3 \\times 10^8} \\approx 3.36 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{ T (or Wb/m}^2\\text{)}$.\n\nComparing these results to the options, Option C is the closest match.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
