    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10380",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A sinusoidal voltage having a frequency of 1 MHz and a peak value of 10 volts is applied to the plates of a parallel plate capacitor which are 2 cm. apart. If an electron is released from one plate at an instant when the applied voltage is zero, find the position of the electron at any subsequent time t. Assume that the initial velocity of the electron is 10^6 m/sec in the X-direction, which is perpendicular to the plates. No magnetic field is present.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.40 × 10^7t + 2.23 cos (2π × 10^6t) m\nB. 1.50 × 10^6t - 2.23 sin (2\\pi × 10^6t) m\nC. 1.40 × 10^7t - 2.23 sin (2\\pi × 10^6t) m\nD. 1.50 × 10^6t + 2.23 cos (2π × 10^6t) m\nE. 1.50 × 10^7t - 2.23 tan (2π × 10^6t) m\nF. 1.50 × 10^6t + 2.23 sin (2π × 10^6t) m\nG. 1.60 × 10^7t - 2.00 sin (2π × 10^6t) m\nH. 1.50 × 10^7t - 1.23 sin (2π × 10^6t) m\nI. 1.50 × 10^7t - 2.23 cos (2\\pi × 10^6t) m\nJ. 1.50 × 10^7t - 2.23 sin (2\\pi × 10^6t) m\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the position of the electron, we start with the electric field $E(t) = \\frac{V_0 \\sin(\\omega t)}{d}$, where $V_0 = 10\\text{ V}$, $d = 0.02\\text{ m}$, and $\\omega = 2\\pi \\times 10^6\\text{ rad/s}$.\nThe acceleration of the electron is $a(t) = \\frac{F}{m} = \\frac{-e E(t)}{m} = \\frac{-e V_0 \\sin(\\omega t)}{md}$.\nIntegrating $a(t)$ once for velocity $v(t)$ (given $v_0 = 10^6\\text{ m/s}$) and again for position $x(t)$ (given $x_0 = 0$):\n$v(t) = v_0 + \\int_0^t \\frac{-e V_0 \\sin(\\omega \\tau)}{md} d\\tau = v_0 + \\frac{e V_0}{md\\omega} (\\cos(\\omega t) - 1)$\n$x(t) = \\int_0^t v(\\tau) d\\tau = (v_0 - \\frac{e V_0}{md\\omega})t + \\frac{e V_0}{md\\omega^2} \\sin(\\omega t)$\nUsing $e \\approx 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\text{ C}$, $m \\approx 9.1 \\times 10^{-31}\\text{ kg}$, $\\omega \\approx 6.28 \\times 10^6\\text{ rad/s}$:\n$\\frac{e V_0}{md\\omega} \\approx \\frac{(1.6 \\times 10^{-19})(10)}{(9.1 \\times 10^{-31})(0.02)(6.28 \\times 10^6)} \\approx 1.40 \\times 10^7\\text{ m/s}$\n$\\frac{e V_0}{md\\omega^2} \\approx \\frac{1.40 \\times 10^7}{6.28 \\times 10^6} \\approx 2.23\\text{ m}$\nThe equation becomes $x(t) = (10^6 - 1.40 \\times 10^7)t + 2.23 \\sin(\\omega t) \\approx -1.30 \\times 10^7t + 2.23 \\sin(\\omega t)$.\nComparing with the options provided (accounting for coordinate direction/sign conventions in typical textbook problems of this type), Option J provides the closest numerical match for the magnitudes.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 522,
