    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the correct answer, we analyze the three parts of the question based on the provided incident amplitude $E = 5\\text{ V/m}$.\n\n1.  **(a) Pure Standing Wave:** A pure standing wave occurs when the amplitude of the reflected wave equals the amplitude of the incident wave ($|E_1| = E$). For a boundary surface (like a perfect conductor), the reflected wave is typically inverted, so $E_1 = -E = -5\\text{ V/m}$.\n2.  **(b) Standing Wave Ratio (VSWR):** The VSWR is calculated as $S = \\frac{E + E_1}{E - E_1}$. Given $E = 5$ and $E_1 = 1/10 = 0.1$, we have $S = \\frac{5 + 0.1}{5 - 0.1} = \\frac{5.1}{4.9} \\approx 1.04$.\n3.  **(c) $E_{max}$ and $E_{min}$:** These are found using $E_{max} = E + E_1$ and $E_{min} = E - E_1$. Given $E = 5$ and $E_1 = 3/5 = 0.6$, we get $E_{max} = 5 + 0.6 = 5.6\\text{ V/m}$ and $E_{min} = 5 - 0.6 = 4.4\\text{ V/m}$.\n\nComparing these results to the options, Option C matches all calculated values.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
