  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10925",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 15 hp internal-combustion engine runs at 1400 rpm and rotates a grinding disc at 700 rpm under smooth conditions. If a triple strand no. 50 roller chain is to transmit power, calculate the number of teeth on both sprockets and the kind of lubrication needed for rust-proof operation.\n\nOptions: \nA. Driving sprocket = 11 teeth, Driven sprocket = 22 teeth, Lubrication type = B\nB. Driving sprocket = 7 teeth, Driven sprocket = 14 teeth, Lubrication type = D\nC. Driving sprocket = 6 teeth, Driven sprocket = 12 teeth, Lubrication type = C\nD. Driving sprocket = 14 teeth, Driven sprocket = 28 teeth, Lubrication type = A\nE. Driving sprocket = 15 teeth, Driven sprocket = 30 teeth, Lubrication type = D\nF. Driving sprocket = 12 teeth, Driven sprocket = 24 teeth, Lubrication type = C\nG. Driving sprocket = 9 teeth, Driven sprocket = 18 teeth, Lubrication type = B\nH. Driving sprocket = 13 teeth, Driven sprocket = 26 teeth, Lubrication type = A\nI. Driving sprocket = 8 teeth, Driven sprocket = 16 teeth, Lubrication type = C\nJ. Driving sprocket = 10 teeth, Driven sprocket = 20 teeth, Lubrication type = A\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the correct answer, we first determine the speed ratio between the engine and the grinding disc:\n$\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{1400\\text{ rpm}}{700\\text{ rpm}} = 2:1$.\nTherefore, the number of teeth on the driven sprocket must be twice the number of teeth on the driving sprocket ($N_{\\text{driven}} = 2 \\times N_{\\text{driving}}$). All options follow this ratio.\n\nIn chain drive design for internal-combustion engines, the minimum recommended number of teeth on the driving sprocket to avoid excessive wear and noise is typically between 13 and 25; however, for specific industrial standards in textbook problems of this type, the combination of 15 and 30 teeth is a standard selection for the given power (15 hp) and speed. Regarding lubrication, \"Type D\" (often referring to oil baths or specific forced-feed systems) is required for high-speed, rust-proof operation in industrial machinery.\n\nComparing the options, Option E provides a reasonable sprocket size and the appropriate high-grade lubrication for the specified conditions.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
