  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10083",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 10-kva, 440/110-volt, 60-cps, single-phase transformer has an efficiency of 97.5% at a rated load of unity power factor. The full-load copper loss is 150 watts, and the core loss is 100 watts. Determine the kilovolt-ampere rating as a 550/440-volt autotransformer, and (b) the efficiency when supplying full load at 80% lagging power factor.\n\nOptions: \nA. 50kva and 99.38%\nB. 55kva and 95.5%\nC. 58kva and 93.8%\nD. 65kva and 94%\nE. 48kva and 96.5%\nF. 52kva and 98%\nG. 40kva and 97%\nH. 45kva and 98.5%\nI. 60kva and 90%\nJ. 70kva and 92%\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the kVA rating of the autotransformer, we use the formula for an autotransformer connected to increase the voltage from $V_1$ to $V_2$. The original transformer rating is $S_{2T} = 10\\text{ kVA}$.\nThe autotransformer rating is $S_{auto} = S_{2T} \\times \\frac{V_H}{V_H - V_L} = 10 \\times \\frac{550}{550 - 440} = 10 \\times \\frac{550}{110} = 50\\text{ kVA}$.\n\nTo find the efficiency at 80% lagging power factor:\n1. Output Power $P_{out} = S_{auto} \\times \\text{pf} = 50,000 \\times 0.8 = 40,000\\text{ W}$.\n2. Total losses are the sum of core loss ($100\\text{ W}$) and full-load copper loss ($150\\text{ W}$), totaling $250\\text{ W}$.\n3. Efficiency $\\eta = \\frac{P_{out}}{P_{out} + \\text{Losses}} = \\frac{40,000}{40,000 + 250} = \\frac{40,000}{40,250} \\approx 99.378\\%$.\n\nMatching these results to the options, we find $50\\text{ kVA}$ and $99.38\\%$.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 321,
        "output_tokens": 364,
        "total_tokens": 685
      },
