  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10298",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 6-pole 3-phase 60-cycle alternator has 12 slots per pole and four conductors per slot. The winding is five-sixths pitch. There are 2,500,000 maxwells (= 0.025 weber) entering the armature from each north pole, and this flux is sinusoidally distributed along the air gap. The armature coils are all connected in series. The winding is Y-connected. Determine the open-circuit emf of the alternator.\n\nOptions: \nA. 296 volts\nB. 480 volts\nC. 425 volts\nD. 350 volts\nE. 550 volts\nF. 600 volts\nG. 513 volts\nH. 250 volts\nI. 275 volts\nJ. 700 volts\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the open-circuit emf of the alternator, we use the emf equation for an alternator:\n$E_{ph} = 4.44 \\cdot f \\cdot \\phi \\cdot N_{ph} \\cdot k_p \\cdot k_d$\n\n1.  **Parameters**:\n    *   Frequency $f = 60\\text{ Hz}$\n    *   Flux $\\phi = 0.025\\text{ Wb}$\n    *   Total conductors $= \\text{Poles} \\times \\text{Slots/Pole} \\times \\text{Conductors/Slot} = 6 \\times 12 \\times 4 = 288$\n    *   Conductors per phase $N_{ph\\_cond} = 288 / 3 = 96$; therefore, turns per phase $N_{ph} = 96 / 2 = 48$\n    *   Pitch factor $k_p = \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{6}) = \\cos(15^\\circ) \\approx 0.966$\n    *   Distribution factor $k_d = \\frac{\\sin(m\\alpha/2)}{m\\sin(\\alpha/2)}$ where $m = \\text{slots/pole/phase} = 12/3 = 4$ and $\\alpha = 180^\\circ/12 = 15^\\circ$.\n        $k_d = \\frac{\\sin(4 \\times 15^\\circ/2)}{4\\sin(15^\\circ/2)} = \\frac{\\sin(30^\\circ)}{4\\sin(7.5^\\circ)} \\approx \\frac{0.5}{4 \\times 0.1305} \\approx 0.957$\n\n2.  **Calculation**:\n    *   $E_{ph} = 4.44 \\times 60 \\times 0.025 \\times 48 \\times 0.966 \\times 0.957 \\approx 296.2\\text{ V}$\n    *   Since the alternator is Y-connected, the line-to-line voltage is $E_L = \\sqrt{3} \\times E_{ph} \\approx 1.732 \\times 296.2 \\approx 513\\text{ V}$\n\nComparing this to the options provided, 513 volts is the result.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
      "success": true,
