    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10287",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Copper normally solidifies at a temperature of 1356°K, but canexist in the super-cooled liquid form up to a temperature of1120°K, after which it starts solidifying spontaneously. Determine the entropy change for the solidification of copper atits temperature of spontaneous solidification (1120°K). Use the following data to solve the problem: Data forCu(1)\\rightleftharpoons Cu(s) CpCu(1)= 7.50 cal deg^-1 mole^-1 C_p Cu(s) = 5.41 + 1.50 × 10^-3T cal deg^-1 mole^-1 \\DeltaH^0 = - 3100 cal\n\nOptions: \nA. - 2.50 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\nB. - 2.09 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\nC. - 2.23 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\nD. - 1.95 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\nE. - 2.28 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\nF. - 3.10 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\nG. 3.00 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\nH. 2.09 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\nI. 0.046 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\nJ. - 1.75 cal deg^-1 mole^-1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the entropy change ($\\Delta S$) for the solidification of copper at $T = 1120\\text{ K}$, we use the relationship between enthalpy and entropy.\n\n1.  **Calculate $\\Delta H$ at $1120\\text{ K}$**: Using Kirchhoff's law, $\\Delta H_{1120} = \\Delta H_{1356} + \\int_{1356}^{1120} \\Delta C_p \\, dT$.\n    $\\Delta C_p = C_{p(s)} - C_{p(l)} = (5.41 + 1.50 \\times 10^{-3}T) - 7.50 = -2.09 + 1.50 \\times 10^{-3}T$.\n    $\\Delta H_{1120} = -3100 + \\int_{1356}^{1120} (-2.09 + 1.50 \\times 10^{-3}T) \\, dT$\n    $\\Delta H_{1120} = -3100 + [(-2.09)(1120 - 1356) + \\frac{1.50 \\times 10^{-3}}{2}(1120^2 - 1356^2)]$\n    $\\Delta H_{1120} = -3100 + [493.24 - 414.38] = -3100 + 78.86 = -3021.14 \\text{ cal/mol}$.\n\n2.  **Calculate $\\Delta S$ at $1120\\text{ K}$**: Since the transition is spontaneous at $1120\\text{ K}$ but the equilibrium temperature is $1356\\text{ K}$, the entropy change is calculated by integrating $\\frac{\\Delta C_p}{T}$ and adding the equilibrium entropy $\\Delta S_{1356} = \\frac{\\Delta H_{1356}}{1356}$.\n    $\\Delta S_{1120} = \\frac{-3100}{1356} + \\int_{1356}^{1120} \\frac{-2.09 + 1.50 \\times 10^{-3}T}{T} \\, dT$\n    $\\Delta S_{1120} = -2.286 + [-2.09 \\ln(\\frac{1120}{1356}) + 1.50 \\times 10^{-3}(1120 - 1356)]$\n    $\\Delta S_{1120} = -2.286 + [0.395 - 0.354] = -2.286 + 0.041 = -2.245 \\text{ cal deg}^{-1} \\text{ mole}^{-1}$.\n\nComparing this result to the options, it is closest to -2.23.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
