    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10864",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider an example of forced convection within a pipe. The local heat transfer coefficient is valid at the point it is evaluated at, and in its immediate vicinity. When the length of pipe as a whole is of concern, the average heat transfer coefficient applies. The average temperature difference is defined as (T_s - T)_av= [{{(T_s - T_in) + (T_s - T_out)}} / 2] where T_s = wall surface temperature T_in = bulk fluid temperature at entrance T_out = bulk fluid temperature at exit. Derive the average heat transfer coefficienth_avin terms of the local coefficienth_x.\n\nOptions: \nA. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0h_x(T_s - T)dx}} / 2\\piR^ L]\nB. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0h_x(T_s - T)dx}} / (T_s -T_av)]\nC. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0h_xdx}} / (T_s - T_av)]\nD. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0h_x(T_s - T)^2dx}} / (T_s - T_av)]\nE. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0h_x(T_s - T)dx}} / (T_s + T_av)]\nF. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0h_x(T_s - T)dx}} / (T_in - T_out)]\nG. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0(h_x + T)dx}} / (T_s - T_av)]\nH. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0h_x(T_s - T)dx}} / {{(T_s - T_in) + (T_s - T_out)}}]\nI. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0h_x(T_s + T)dx}} / (T_s -T_av)]\nJ. h_av= [{{^L\\int_0h_x(T_s - T)dx}} / L]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the average heat transfer coefficient $h_{av}$, we start by equating the total heat transfer rate $Q$ calculated using the local coefficient $h_x$ and the average coefficient $h_{av}$.\n\n1. The total heat transfer rate is the integral of the local heat flux over the surface area:\n   $Q = \\int_0^L h_x (T_s - T) (2\\pi R) dx$\n2. By definition, the total heat transfer can also be expressed using the average heat transfer coefficient and the average temperature difference:\n   $Q = h_{av} (T_s - T_{av}) (2\\pi R L)$\n3. Equating the two expressions and solving for $h_{av}$:\n   $h_{av} (T_s - T_{av}) (2\\pi R L) = \\int_0^L h_x (T_s - T) (2\\pi R) dx$\n   $h_{av} = \\frac{\\int_0^L h_x (T_s - T) dx}{L(T_s - T_{av})}$ (Wait, looking at the provided options, the $L$ is often absorbed or handled differently, but the core physics requires the integral of $h_x \\Delta T$ divided by the average $\\Delta T$ and length). \n\nComparing this to the options, Option B represents the ratio of the integral of the local heat flux (normalized by perimeter) to the average temperature difference. Note: In many textbook contexts for this specific derivation, the $L$ is omitted in the denominator of the option or implied within the average definition. Option B is the closest physical representation of $h_{av} = \\frac{\\int h_x \\Delta T dx}{L \\Delta T_{av}}$ if we assume the integral is normalized or $L$ is part of the temperature average context.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
