    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10929",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A signal source operating at 50MHz has an output impedance of 20 ohms. It is to supply power through a coaxial line to a load impedance of 150 + j40 ohms. Is it possible to design a quarter-wave transformer to match the source to the load?\n\nOptions: \nA. A quarter-wave transformer with a characteristic impedance of 100 ohms can match the source to the load\nB. The matching transformer would need a characteristic impedance = √[(20)(150)] = 54.77 ohms\nC. The matching transformer would have a characteristic impedance = \\surd[(50)(150)] = 86.60 ohms\nD. A quarter-wave transformer cannot provide perfect impedance matching\nE. A quarter-wave transformer is unnecessary since the source and load impedances are already matched\nF. The load impedance can be made real only if a series inductor of - j40 ohms is added\nG. A quarter-wave transformer can provide perfect impedance matching\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To design a quarter-wave transformer for impedance matching, the load impedance must be purely resistive (real). In this case, the load impedance is $Z_L = 150 + j40\\ \\Omega$, which contains a reactive component ($j40\\ \\Omega$). A standard quarter-wave transformer can only match a real source impedance to a real load impedance; it cannot cancel out the reactance of the load. Therefore, it cannot provide a perfect match without additional matching components (like a shunt or series stub) to first eliminate the imaginary part.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
