    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Food supplements, including trace minerals and vitamins are frequently advertised with promising health benefits. Which of the following substance could be consumed in excess, i.e. well above the recommended daily requirement?\n\n\nOptions: \nA. Vitamin C\nB. Vitamin D\nC. Zinc\nD. Vitamin A\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **A**.\n\nVitamin C is a water-soluble vitamin, meaning that excess amounts are generally excreted through urine rather than stored in the body, giving it a much higher threshold for toxicity compared to fat-soluble vitamins. In contrast, Vitamins A and D are fat-soluble and can accumulate to toxic levels in the liver and fatty tissues, while excess Zinc can interfere with copper absorption and cause neurological issues. Therefore, Vitamin C is the substance among the choices that can be consumed in the largest relative excess with the lowest risk of severe acute toxicity.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
