    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5015",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A new blood test to detect prostate cancer is evaluated in 300 male volunteers. A needle biopsy of the prostate gland is done on all men with serum prostate-specific antigen concentrations greater than 5 ng/mL (N<4). One hundred men undergo biopsy procedures; 90 are found to have prostate cancer, and five are found to have chronic prostatitis. Which of the following is necessary to calculate the sensitivity of this test?\n\nOptions: \nA. Prevalence of chronic prostatitis in the general population\nB. Prevalence of prostate cancer in the general population\nC. Prostate biopsies of men with test results greater than 5 ng/mL\nD. Prostate biopsies of men with test results equal to or below 5 ng/mL\nE. Number of men with test results greater than 5 ng/mL and chronic prostatitis\nF. Incidence of prostate cancer in the general population\nG. Number of men with test results greater than 5 ng/mL and a normal biopsy specimen\nH. Number of men with test results equal to or below 5 ng/mL and a normal biopsy specimen\nI. Number of men with test results equal to or below 5 ng/mL and chronic prostatitis\nJ. Incidence of chronic prostatitis in the general population\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To calculate the sensitivity of a test, one needs the number of True Positives (TP) and False Negatives (FN), as the formula is $\\text{Sensitivity} = \\frac{TP}{TP + FN}$. In this scenario, we know the number of men who tested positive and had cancer (TP), but we do not know how many men who tested negative actually had cancer (FN). Therefore, we must perform biopsies on the men who tested negative (results $\\le 5$ ng/mL) to identify the False Negatives.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
