    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A medially directed strabismus (internal squint) occurs when the lateral rectus muscle is paralyzed, leaving the medial rectus muscle unopposed. The lateral rectus is specifically innervated by the abducens nerve (CN VI). Therefore, damage to this nerve prevents the eye from abducting, causing it to deviate toward the midline.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
