    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5007",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When you examine a patient who has suffered an unfavourable fracture of the body of the mandible, you would expect the\n\nOptions: \nA. anterior fragment to be displaced anteriorly by the action of the lateral pterygoid muscle.\nB. posterior fragment to be displaced downwards by the action of the digastric muscle.\nC. anterior fragment to be displaced backwards by action of the temporalis muscle.\nD. posterior fragment to be displaced laterally by the action of the medial pterygoid muscle.\nE. anterior fragment to be displaced medially by the action of the medial pterygoid muscle.\nF. posterior fragment to be displaced upwards by the action of the masseter muscle.\nG. posterior fragment to be displaced anteriorly by the action of the lateral pterygoid muscle.\nH. anterior fragment to be displaced upwards by the action of the masseter muscle.\nI. anterior fragment to be displaced downwards by the action of the digastric muscle.\nJ. posterior fragment to be displaced medially by action of the medial pterygoid muscle.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In an unfavorable fracture of the mandibular body, the displacement is determined by the pull of the attached muscles. The **posterior fragment** is typically displaced **upwards** by the elevator muscles (masseter, temporalis, and medial pterygoid) and **medially** by the medial pterygoid. The **anterior fragment** is typically displaced **downwards and backwards** by the suprahyoid muscles (such as the digastric).\n\nBased on the options provided, the most accurate description of the displacement in an unfavorable fracture is that the posterior fragment is pulled superiorly.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
