    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Constricting the efferent arteriole increases the resistance to outflow from the glomerular capillaries, which leads to a \"backup\" of blood and a subsequent increase in glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure ($P_{GC}$). This increase in hydrostatic pressure is the primary mechanism that drives an increase in the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) under these conditions, provided the constriction is not so severe that it drastically reduces renal blood flow. Therefore, the most direct change among the Starling forces is an increase in hydrostatic pressure.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
