      "generated_answer": "The most appropriate rationale for developing a killed (inactivated) vaccine for a newly identified, severe respiratory illness is safety, specifically the elimination of the risk that the vaccine strain could mutate back into a pathogenic form. Unlike live-attenuated vaccines, killed vaccines contain non-replicating genetic material, making them inherently incapable of causing the disease they are intended to prevent. Therefore, they avoid the safety concerns associated with potential reversion to virulence in the population.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
