    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5129",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 76-year-old man comes to the office because of early awakening at night. He has no difficulty falling asleep but routinely wakes up between 2:00 and 3:00 AM. The patient is a retired postal worker, and he has always been physically active. He has diabetes mellitus controlled by diet. The patient drinks one cup of coffee in the morning with breakfast and usually walks for exercise in the morning. Before retiring at night he has one alcoholic beverage. He has no history of depression, nightmares, or snoring and he takes no over-the-counter medications. His wife of 45 years is also in good health. Vital signs are temperature 37.1°C (98.8°F), pulse 96/min and regular, respirations 18/min, and blood pressure 135/90 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a well-nourished, well-developed man. He is not obese. Examination of the head and neck is normal; there are no bruits or jugular venous distention. Chest is clear, and heart is normal with S1 and S2. Abdomen is soft and nontender with active bowel sounds and no organomegaly. Rectal examination discloses no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most appropriate management of this patient's insomnia?\n\nOptions: \nA. Recommend the patient to watch television before going to bed.\nB. Prescribe sleeping pills\nC. Advise the patient to read and snack in bed to relax\nD. Recommend a daytime nap to compensate for lost sleep\nE. Prescribe a vigorous pre-bedtime exercise regimen\nF. Prescribe sertraline\nG. Prescribe a low-carb diet\nH. Advise the patient to discontinue his bedtime drink of alcohol\nI. Advise the patient to drink alcohol earlier in the day\nJ. Advise the patient to increase his intake of caffeine\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The patient is experiencing early awakening, a common characteristic of alcohol-induced sleep fragmentation. While alcohol can help with sleep onset (falling asleep), its metabolism during the night leads to a \"rebound effect\" that disrupts sleep maintenance and causes early awakening. Therefore, the most appropriate management is to eliminate alcohol consumption shortly before bedtime.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
