    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **\\boxed{A}**.\n\nCarbon monoxide (CO) is diffusion-limited because it binds to hemoglobin (Hb) with extremely high affinity, which prevents the partial pressure of CO in the pulmonary capillary blood from rising significantly. As a result, a large partial pressure gradient between the alveoli and the blood is maintained throughout the entire length of the capillary, meaning the amount of CO transferred depends solely on the diffusion properties of the membrane. In contrast, perfusion-limited gases reach partial pressure equilibrium quickly.",
