    "global index": "MedMCQA_2515",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a reaction substrate is available in a concentration that is l000times the Km value of the enzyme. After 9 minutes of reaction, l7o substrate is conveed to product (12 microgram/ml). If the concentration of the enzyme is changed to 1/3 and concentration of substrate is doubled. What is the time taken to conve the substrate into the same amount of product, i.e. I 2microgram/ml?\n\nOptions: \nA. 9 minutes\nB. 4.5 minutes\nC. 27 minutes\nD. 13.5 minutes\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the time taken, we analyze the enzyme kinetics using the Michaelis-Menten equation: $v = \\frac{V_{max}[S]}{K_m + [S]}$.\n\n1.  **Initial State:** Since $[S] = 1000 \\times K_m$, the enzyme is saturated, meaning $v \\approx V_{max}$. The rate $V_{max}$ is proportional to the enzyme concentration $[E]$, so $v_1 \\propto [E]$.\n2.  **Final State:** The substrate concentration is doubled, but since it was already $1000 \\times K_m$, the enzyme remains saturated; thus, the rate depends only on $[E]$. When $[E]$ is changed to $1/3$, the new rate $v_2 = \\frac{1}{3} v_1$.\n3.  **Calculation:** To produce the same amount of product, the time required is inversely proportional to the rate: $t_2 = t_1 \\times \\frac{v_1}{v_2} = 9 \\text{ minutes} \\times 3 = 27 \\text{ minutes}$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 200,
