    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **D**.\n\nIn nutritional or genetic forms of osteomalacia (such as Vitamin D deficiency), serum phosphate levels are typically low (hypophosphatemia) due to secondary hyperparathyroidism. In contrast, renal osteodystrophy is primarily driven by chronic kidney disease, where the kidneys fail to excrete phosphate, leading to **hyperphosphatemia**. This elevated phosphate level is a hallmark feature that distinguishes renal-related bone disease from other forms of osteomalacia.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
