    "global index": "MedMCQA_2301",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Cell membrane of a cell is freely permeable to three ions X, Y and Z. At resting membrane potential, the respective equilibrium potentials of Xand Y are -30mV and -40 mV.What will happen to the membrane potential if a drug is infused which can block the permeability of Z?\n\nOptions: \nA. Depolarisation\nB. Hyperpolarization\nC. No change\nD. Hyperpolarization following repolarization\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the effect of blocking ion Z, we must consider the relationship between the resting membrane potential ($V_m$) and the equilibrium potentials ($E_{ion}$) of the permeable ions. According to the Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation, $V_m$ is a weighted average of the equilibrium potentials of all permeable ions; since $E_X$ (-30 mV) and $E_Y$ (-40 mV) are relatively depolarized compared to a typical resting potential, ion Z must have a very negative equilibrium potential (like $K^+$) to maintain a standard resting state. If the permeability to the most hyperpolarizing ion (Z) is blocked, the membrane potential will shift toward the more positive equilibrium potentials of X and Y, resulting in depolarization.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
