    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_191",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following scales or chords can be played on the harp using a glissando technique?\n①C whole tone scale\n②Ab Ionian mode\n⓷a minor harmonic D-VII7 chord\n④G major D7 chord\n⑤Chromatic scale\n\nOptions: \nA. ①②③\nB. ①②③④\nC. ①③④\nD. ②③④⑤\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine which scales or chords can be played as a glissando on a pedal harp, one must consider the harp's construction: it has seven strings per octave, each controlled by a pedal that can set the string to flat, natural, or sharp.\n\n1.  **C whole tone scale (①):** Possible by tuning strings to C, D, E, F#, G#, A#, and B# (enharmonic to C).\n2.  **Ab Ionian mode (②):** Possible as it is a standard 7-note diatonic scale (Ab, Bb, C, Db, Eb, F, G).\n3.  **a minor harmonic D-VII7 chord (③):** Possible by using enharmonic equivalents to create a four-note chord spread across seven strings (e.g., G#, B, D, F).\n4.  **G major D7 chord (④):** Possible by tuning strings to G, A, B, C, D, E, and F# (or using enharmonic doublings for a pure D7 sound).\n5.  **Chromatic scale (⑤):** **Impossible** to play as a single-motion glissando because the harp only has seven strings per octave and pedals cannot be shifted fast enough during the slide to cover all 12 semitones.\n\nBased on this, options ①, ②, ③, and ④ are possible.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
