    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In Chinese pentatonic music theory, the **B Shang** (商) mode is based on the **A Gong** (宫) system (since Shang is the second degree, A-B-C#-E-F#). A hexatonic (six-tone) scale is formed by adding either the *Qingyu* (清羽, the minor 6th) or the *Bianzhi* (变徵, the augmented 4th) to the pentatonic scale. In the A Gong system, these added notes are **G** and **D#** (or **D** and **G#** depending on the specific traditional scale structure, but typically D# and G in the context of the B Shang scale's relationship to its parent A Gong).\n\nBased on the standard application of hexatonic additions (adding the 4th or 7th of the parent scale):\nThe parent scale is A Gong (A, B, C#, E, F#).\nThe two common additional notes are **D** (natural 4th) or **G** (natural 7th).\n\nThe correct letter choice is \\boxed{B}.",
