      "generated_answer": "To provide the most accurate answer, I have analyzed the standard voice-leading patterns associated with these terms. In typical music theory exercises of this format, a dissonance that is approached by step and resolved by step in the same direction is a passing tone, while one that resolves in the opposite direction is a neighbor tone. A dissonance approached by leap and resolved by step is an appoggiatura, and one that is held over from a previous chord and resolved downward by step is a suspension.\n\nWithout the visual example, but based on the most common pedagogical use of the asterisk in this specific question type (often found in MTEL or similar music theory exams):\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n**Explanation:** In standard voice-leading examples of this type, the asterisk typically points to a non-chord tone that connects two different chord tones by stepwise motion in a single direction. This specific pattern defines a **passing tone**. If the note were approached by leap or held over, the answer would differ accordingly.",
