      "generated_answer": "To find the inversion of an interval, we subtract the interval number from 16 (for compound intervals) or 9 (for simple intervals) and reverse the quality. A diminished fourteenth (d14) is a compound interval; its simple equivalent is a diminished seventh (d7), and its inversion is an augmented second (A2).\n\n1.  The inversion of a **diminished fourteenth** is an **augmented second** (15 - 14 = 1, but in standard inversion theory, $16 - 14 = 2$).\n2.  Options A, B, and D are all forms of an augmented second (simple, octave-displaced, or compound).\n3.  An **augmented seventh** (Option C) is the inversion of a diminished second, not a diminished fourteenth.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
