      "generated_answer": "In the Pythagorean major scale of F-sharp ($F\\sharp, G\\sharp, A\\sharp, B, C\\sharp, D\\sharp, E\\sharp, F\\sharp$), the \"apotomes\" refer to the chromatic semitones that occur on the notes requiring sharps to distinguish them from the natural diatonic steps. In this specific scale, the two pitches that function as the raised chromatic intervals (apotomes) relative to the Pythagorean tuning system are $B\\sharp$ and $F\\sharp$. These represent the specific ratios defined by the Pythagorean tuning of the scale's leading tones and tonic.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
