    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When transposing a G major melody written on the bass clef, which of the following methods does not require changing the position of the notes?\n\nOptions: \nA. Keep the clef unchanged, and transpose to a higher minor second\nB. Transpose to F major, and change to use the alto clef\nC. Transpose to D major, and change to use the treble clef\nD. Transpose to a lower major second, and change to use the treble clef\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine which method keeps the notes in the same position on the staff, we must compare the pitch names associated with the lines and spaces of different clefs.\n\n1.  **Bass Clef (G major):** The bottom line is G2.\n2.  **Alto Clef (F major):** The bottom line is F3. If a melody is transposed down a major second from G to F and the clef is changed to alto, the note originally on the bottom line (G) becomes an F, which occupies the same bottom-line position in the alto clef.\n3.  **Treble Clef (D major):** The bottom line is E4. Transposing from G major to D major (up a fifth) and changing to treble clef would place the new tonic (D) on the space below the staff, changing the visual positions.\n\nOption B is the correct choice because transposing down a major second (G to F) while switching to the alto clef (where every note name is shifted down by one position relative to the bass clef) results in the notes remaining on the same lines and spaces.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
